\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 My introduction to fantasy began when I was assigned J.R.R. Tolkien\rquote s }{\i\fs24 The Hobbit}{\fs24 
 in my 6th grade reading class. Although I was only eleven at the time, I was already a fan of science fiction\emdash I\rquote d read a lot of Robert Heinlein\rquote s juvenile SF, as well as Doc Smith\rquote 
s Lensman books and a smattering of other titles. I hadn\rquote t read anything in the fantasy genre, and as I recall, I had little desire to do so. }{\i\fs24 The Hobbit}{\fs24  changed that for me, and I went back and re\_
read it as soon as I finished turning the last page. After that, I was off and running. The next thing I read was the }{\i\fs24 Lord of the Rings}{\fs24 . Then I found Terry Brooks\rquote  }{\i\fs24 The Sword of Shannara}{\fs24 
, followed by Ursula K. Leguin\rquote s }{\i\fs24 A Wizard of Earthsea}{\fs24 .
\par \tab And that\rquote s where I was brought to a screeching halt. My hometown library was on the small side, and all of their fantasy and science fiction books occupied one carousel in the kids\rquote 
 section. It may be hard to believe now, but even as recently as 1977 fantasy was a poor cousin to science fiction, which was a poor cousin to mainstream fiction. Bookstores just didn\rquote t have the massive sections devoted to f
antasy and science fiction that they have today. By hook and by crook, I eventually tracked down more and more fantasy titles. But when I was still desperately searching for fantastic fiction, sometime in 1978 or 1979, I ran across a brand\_
new game called Dungeons & Dragons\'ae. So, like many people, I came to fantasy role\_playing through my love of fantasy fiction.
\par \tab In writing this book, I\rquote ve come to the conclusion that the single defining characteristic of the fantasy genre is magic. Every fantasy story feat
ures a character who can use magic, owns a magical item, or is confronted with a magical situation or paradox. Movies like }{\i\fs24 Ivanhoe }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 Robin Hood}{\fs24  are wonderful adventures, but they\rquote re not fantasy stories\emdash 
there\rquote s no magic. On the other hand, the popular Star
 Wars movies are fantasy, not science fiction, because magic (referred to as the Force) is part of the story. Science fiction is the literature of things that could happen; fantasy is the literature of things that can\rquote 
t happen. And magic is the very essence of the impossible.
\par \tab So, here\rquote s a book about magic in the AD&D\'ae game. I\rquote 
ve tried to include enough options and choices to give you, the reader, the ability to give magic in your particular campaign almost any kind of flavor or feel that you like. If you want to make magic rarer, more \ldblquote realistic,\rdblquote 
 or more dangerous, you can find rules in here for doing so. If you want rules to add detail and complexity to the AD&D magic system, they\rquote re here. Or if you just want some new wizard and priest character types as 
well as some complementary spells, you\rquote ll find them. When I started this project, I thought that I\rquote d have a hard time filling this whole book . . . now, I can\rquote t believe how much more I could have written if space had permitted.
\par \tab Have fun, and good gaming!
\par 
\par 
\par \tab Rich Baker
\par \tab January, 1996
\par \tab Lake Geneva, Wisconsin
