\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs36\cf6 Introduction}{\fs36 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 What\rquote s a fantasy game without magic?
\par \tab Sure, the AD&D\'ae game can be played without spellcasters, enchanted monsters, or magical items. Everyone can still role\_
play brave heroes, confront deadly foes, and attempt great quests or deeds of mythical proportions. In fact, it can be fun and challenging to do so in a nonmagical setting. But the point remains that magic, more than any ot
her characteristic, defines the AD&D game. As a }{\i\fs24 fantasy}{\fs24  role\_
playing game, AD&D is anchored in the traditions of fantasy literature, and fantasy literature by definition features some element of magic. Even if the heroes of a fantasy story distrust or dislike magic, it\rquote 
s still there in the background as part of the world they live in.
\par \tab Generally, most writers of fantasy literature create their own unique systems of magic to explain the supernatural powers their heroes and villains employ. With great care an
d deliberate effort, authors define what magic can and cannot do in their worlds. For example, in }{\i\fs24 The Lord of the Rings}{\fs24 , J.R.R. Tolkien circumscribes the wizard Gandalf\rquote s power by placing restrictions on Gandalf\rquote 
s freedom of action. The great wizard is bound by a code of secrecy and noninterference that prevents him from directly challenging the power of Sauron, and he can only help and advise as the Free Peoples of Middle\_
earth fight their own battles. Jack Vance\rquote s }{\i\fs24 Dying Earth}{\fs24  stories assume that wizards must study complicated patterns and formulae to memorize very specific spells that may only be used once before vanishing from the wizard\rquote 
s memory. (Sound familiar?)
\par \tab There are very good reasons for limiting magic\rquote s power from a literary point of view. Modern readers need to see real challenges and obstacles for the characters in a story, and magic systems that are too open\_ended can wreck a story\rquote 
s credibility and sense of suspense. If Gandalf could have just }{\i\fs24 wished}{\fs24  the One Ring into the fires of Mount Doom from Frodo\rquote s living room, what would have been the point of the trek to Mordor? Since fantasy role\_
playing games are flexible models of the fantasy genre, the same considerations are true for them. A fantasy RPG has to set very precise rules for how magic
 works and what it can do, and the presentation and workings of the magic system inevitably become the game\rquote s salient characteristics.
\par 
\par 
\par \tab In fantasy literature, it\rquote s common for a magic system to be defined for only one world\_setting at a time by a single author (although shared\_world concepts are fairly common, too). The AD&D game\rquote 
s magic system represents a common framework built up by hundreds of designers, Dungeon Masters, and players over more than twenty years. There are }{\i\fs24 thousands }{\fs24 of spells and magical items defined\emdash 
a volume of material that is an order of magnitude larger than any other magic system in games or fiction. The basic assumptions of what magic is and how it works in the AD&D game are shared by literally millio
ns of gamers and fantasy fans. Despite the immense importance of magic to the game, it is one of the few areas that has remained nearly unchanged to date in the evolution of the game. Magic never changed in function; it simply grew amoeba\_
like, adding more and more spells and items while the basic, underlying assumptions remained the same.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option\'99: Spells & Magic }{\fs24 examines the AD&D magic system from every angle. First, the spellcasting classes\emdash wizards, priests, and less dedicated magic wielders such as bards or rangers\emdash 
will be examined in detail. The various schools and spheres of spells are reorganized and new class abilities are introduced, along with an optional point\_based character class design system compatible with the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Skills
 & Powers }{\fs24 
rulebook. New proficiencies and detailed information about wizard and priest equipment adds depth and variety to any campaign. A new magic memorization and casting system is introduced in Chapter 6, providing new ways to customize a character\rquote s s
pell selection. Spells in combat and critical hits with spells are detailed in Chapter 8. And last, but not least, there are more than 30 pages of new spells included in this book.
\par \tab Like any of the Player\rquote s Option books, the material in this supplement is optional. The DM is free to use as much or as little of }{\i\fs24 Spells & Magic }{\fs24 
as he wishes to in his campaign. However, we have tried to present systems that do not contradict each other, so it is possible to use all the rules additions and expansions without any difficulty.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 What You Need to Use This Book}{\fs24 
\par \tab At a minimum, you should have access to a }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master\'ae Guide }{\fs24 to make use of this rules expansion. Note that }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Spells & Magic }{\fs24 
is compatible with the previous books in the Player\rquote s Option line; character class design rules in this book are expansions of the class design rules from }{\i\fs24 Skills & Powers}{\fs24 
, and the chapter on spells in combat is tailored for use with the }{\i\fs24 Combat & Tactics}{\fs24  skirmish system. In addition, the }{\i\fs24 Tome of Magic }{\fs24 is referred to a number of times in this book; you don\rquote t need }{\i\fs24 
Tome of Magic }{\fs24 to use this book, but it adds many spells and magical items you may find useful in your campaign.
\par }{\i\fs24 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Integrating }{\i\fs28\cf12 Spells & Magic }{\fs28\cf12 into Your Campaign  }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Spells & Magic }{\fs24 i
s designed principally as an expansion, not a replacement. However, there are a few special cases where the information presented here should be taken as an update and replacement of existing rules. Specifically, the wizard spell schools and priest spell 
spheres have been slightly rearranged to improve game balance and make the wizard and priest specialist classes more competitive with respect to the general versions of these characters, the mage and the cleric.
\par \tab Most of the rest of this book can be integrated piecemeal into an existing campaign without any trouble. For example, additional proficiencies and new spells can be approved or disapproved by the DM on a case\_by\_
case basis. These were designed to be completely usable with or without the Player\rquote s Option rules.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Making the Switch}{\fs24 
\par \tab Adding spells, magical items, or the critical hit rules to an existing campaign is fairly easy, but a DM may have trouble with some other material in this book. In particular, exist
ing spellcasters may wish to take advantage of new class abilities or optional specializations that weren\rquote t available when the character was first created. There are several ways to handle this. First, there\rquote s no reason that a DM couldn
\rquote t allow a player to \ldblquote re\_design\rdblquote  his character, incorporating the abilities he thinks his character should have had all along. If a PC cleric comes from a savage tribe and portrays himself as a barbarian, it\rquote 
s perfectly reasonable to allow him to rebuild his character as a shaman (see Chapter 2) and continue play. If the player is altering his character without any good rationale or explanation for why he\rquote 
s making the change, the DM can require the character to pay a penalty of 10% to 50% of his experience point total, depending on the DM\rquote s assessment of the scope of the alterations.
\par \tab There are a couple of things a player should }{\i\fs24 not}{\fs24  be able to do by redesigning his character. A character shouldn\rquote t change specializations without a very good justification, so a necromancer shouldn\rquote 
t be rebuilt as an enchanter or wild mage, and a specialty priest of Lathander shouldn\rquote t become a priest of Helm. A character\rquote s basic ability scores, equipment, proficiencies, hit points, and general personality shouldn\rquote 
t change. If a player was playing a barbarian cleric correctly, he was probably choosing skills and weapons appropriate for a shaman\emdash and if he wasn\rquote 
t selecting these skills, the reasoning behind the switch becomes much more suspect. Finally, a character shouldn\rquote t actually change classes or become dual\_classed or multi\_classed.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 The Spell Point System: }{\fs24 The most drastic change to the AD&D game lies in the new spell point system described in Chapter 6. Try running a brief \ldblquote trial adventure\rdblquote 
 using the rules before incorporating them into your ca
mpaign. The spell point rules provide spellcasters with a lot more flexibility than the standard magic system without increasing their raw combat or spell power, but if PCs are allowed to make use of spell points, NPCs and monsters should be able to as we
ll.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 The Role of Magic in the Campaign  }{\fs24 It\rquote 
s safe to say that magic in one form or another is present in virtually all AD&D campaigns; only the most historical or unusual settings do away with magic altogether. But, beyond this simple observation, it\rquote s clea
r that each group of AD&D players has their own interpretation of what magic is, how it works, how various spells interact with each other, and what player characters should and should not be able to do with their arsenal of spells and magical items. By a
ltering some of the basic assumptions that are part of the AD&D game\rquote s magic system, a DM can infuse his campaign with its own unique flavor and texture.
\par \tab While the greater portion of this book deals with altering the rules of the game, this isn\rquote t always a 
necessary part of changing the way that the players (and the NPCs they interact with) view magic and its effect on their world. For example, let\rquote s say that the }{\i\fs24 nature }{\fs24 
of magic in a campaign setting can be described by a simple scale that rates the scarcity
, mystery, power, and cost of magic on a scale of 1 to 10. Obviously, a campaign with magic that is extremely scarce and weak in power represents a very mundane world when compared to a world where powerful magic is very common.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Scarcity}{\fs24 
\par \tab How common is magic in the campaign? Are wizards and spell\_wielding priests so rare that even low\_
level characters are figures of legend, or are they so common that any hamlet or crossroads village has its own resident spellcasters? Most AD&D campaigns take a position bet
ween these two extremes, but lean towards the high end of the scale, falling in the 6 to 8 range on the 10\_point scale\emdash wizards and priests appear as allies, enemies, sources of information, or even window\_
dressing in literally every adventure a typical gro
up plays. At the highest extremes, campaigns feature numerous spellcasters and magical items. Even a small town has several wizards of skill, plus dozens of minor merchants, craftsmen, or innkeepers with a spell or two up their sleeve. Wizards are so comm
on that even the most remarkable mages lose their aura of mystery.
\par \tab So, what does this mean? In a normal campaign, it\rquote s safe to assume that anywhere the PCs go, the locals know a nearby wizard, and most people encounter a wizard a couple of times a year. An
y sizable town has at least one or two resident wizards and spell\_using priests, plus a handful of folks with minor magical powers such as herbalists, hedge wizards, and healers. Large towns or small cities may have up to a dozen or so magic\_
using characters, and great cities could support several dozen without crowding. Almost any NPC above 1st level owns one or more magical items, even if they\rquote 
re fairly small or expendable, and player characters frequently own about three to five magical items by the time they reach 4th to 7th level.
\par \tab In campaigns where magic is not as common (say, a 2 to 4 on the scale), the spellcasting characters become truly unique and important. A priest who can actually invoke his deity\rquote s power in the form of spells may be perceived 
by the great clerical hierarchy as a saint or great patriarch in the making, or possibly as a dangerous reminder of the true faith in those hierarchies that have become complacent or corrupt. A high\_
ranking hierarch without spells will certainly watch a low\_level PC cleric very carefully, especially if the PC makes no efforts to hide the \ldblquote miraculous\rdblquote  effects he creates with simple 1st\_ and 2nd\_
level spells. Similarly, if wizard magic is quite scarce, a PC wizard can\rquote t help but gather attention, fame, and not a little fear if he publicly displays his skills.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Priests vs. Wizards: }{\fs24 A wizard\rquote s magic and a priest\rquote s magic are not the same thing, and both forms of magic do not have to be present in a campaign to the same degree. Imagine a world where wizards are vi
ewed as the worst sort of villain and persecuted without remorse for decades. Wizards and their spells might be exceedingly scarce, while priests are far more common because they are socially acceptable\emdash 
thus, the presence of wizard magic might only be a 1 or 2 while priest magic is closer to a 6 or 7 on the scale.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Magical Items: }{\fs24 Similarly, magical items might be more or less common than spellcasters. If no one had ever invented the spell }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 , it\rquote s reasonable to assume that magical items might b
e a rarity in even the most magical campaign settings. The reverse could be true if there was a lost civilization of highly advanced wizards who left behind great numbers of artifacts and items. The wizards of today might be armed to the teeth with magica
l items, despite the fact that they are struggling to grasp the basics of spellcasting.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Mystery}{\fs24 
\par \tab Can anyone in the campaign be a wizard or priest, or do these characters have to belong to a select set in order to even begin their studies? Do the common peo
ple know enough about magic to distinguish between priest and wizard spells? Is the study of magic a study of easily\_
defined natural laws, or are the forms of magic deliberately obscured by generations of needless rite and ceremony? Most importantly, do the PCs know the limits of a spellcaster\rquote s powers?
\par \tab In most AD&D games, characters \ldblquote in the know\rdblquote  have an excellent grasp of exactly what each spell available can do. After all, most players are quite familiar with the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
 and know the spells they can make use of inside and out. But most common NPCs aren\rquote t as knowledgeable; the typical innkeeper doesn\rquote t know that a low\_level wizard can use }{\i\fs24 invisibility }{\fs24 
to walk out without paying his tab, or }{\i\fs24 fool\rquote s gold }{\fs24 to cheat him. He just knows that wizards can do things that ordinary people can\rquote t, and if he\rquote s a bright innkeeper, he never falls for the same trick twice.
\par \tab In a less mysterious world, the same innkeeper knows to look out for }{\i\fs24 invisibility}{\fs24 , }{\i\fs24 charm person, fool\rquote s gold, }{\fs24 and half\_a\_dozen other dirty tric
ks. He may even know enough to request a wizard to relinquish certain spell components to make sure a particularly obnoxious spell (}{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24 , for instance) won\rquote t be available to that wizard while he\rquote s in the innkeeper
\rquote s place of business. In this kind of s
etting, everyone would know that priests can heal injuries, blindness, or disease, or possibly bring back a loved one from the dead, and priest characters will be constantly asked to use their powers on someone\rquote s behalf.
\par \tab In a world where the nature of magic is cloaked in superstition and ignorance\emdash an 8 or 9 on the scale\emdash 
spellcasters will generally inspire fear in anyone who learns of their powers. Note that even the wizard character himself may not really know why his spells work\emdash imagine a character who begins a }{\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 
spell with a thunderous declaration of the names of forbidden powers, just because he was taught to do it that way. Of course, one of the \lquote names\rquote  is actually the spell\rquote 
s verbal component, and the rest of the nonsense has no effect on the casting of the spell. It\rquote s a good idea for a DM with this kind of world to forbid players from looking up spells and effects in the }{\i\fs24 PHB}{\fs24 
, since their characters only have access to a portion of this knowledge. The DM should also feel free to alter standard spell effects and create new spells just to instill a sense of dread and wonder in experienced, jaded players.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Power}{\fs24 
\par \tab What can magic accomplish in the campaign? Is there anything it can\rquote t do, and why? Will 10th\_level magic be allowed as an option, or is 9th level the most powerful magic available? Can }{\i\fs24 wishes }{\fs24 
change history or reverse events that have already occurred? To what degree do the gods and their avatars involve themselves in this world setting? In most magic systems, setting boundaries to a character\rquote 
s ability to affect events with magic is vitally important. Typically, an AD&D game assumes that 9th\_level spells are the most powerful magic known to mortals, and that the player characters encounter a deity no more than once or twice over the cour
se of an entire campaign.
\par \tab The impact of magic on a campaign world can be greatly lessened by reducing the maximum level of spells that can work there, although this is getting into rules alterations. For example, by limiting spells to 8th level, mages can
 no longer make use of }{\i\fs24 wishes }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 gates}{\fs24 
. If the maximum is 7th level, mages lose the spell permanency, which is a key part of the magical item creation process. Without this spell, magical items become temporary or disposable\emdash no persistent enchantments can exist.
\par \tab Another point lies in the emphasis on the power of characters versus the power of magical items. Should a fighter become a killing machine because he happened to find a }{\i\fs24 vorpal blade}{\fs24 
, or should most of his combat bonuses be derived from skills and training? In earlier incarnations, the AD&D game leaned strongly towards the first option, but with the advent of weapon mastery, style specializations, and other character\_
based bonuses, it is now possible to create a character who doesn\rquote t need a powerful magical item to drastically increase his combat power. The real danger to game balance lies in combining these two benefits\emdash 
a weapon master equipped with a powerful magical weapon becomes nearly unstoppable. If your campaign features a lot of high\_powered magic, you should strongly consider playing without optional specialization or proficiency rules.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 The Cost of Magic}{\fs24 
\par \tab In fantasy literature, there is often a price to be paid for magical power. Wizards may have to make terrible pacts with dark powers for the knowledge they seek, priests may have to sacrifice something dear to them to invoke their deity\rquote 
s favor, or the spellcaster may pay an immediate price in terms of fatigue, illness, or even a loss of sanity. Generally, the AD&D game is quite forgiving in
 this regard; when a character casts a spell, he expends a few unusual material components and simply forgets the spell he had known. It\rquote 
s easy to increase the cost of magic by strictly enforcing the requirement to procure material components for spells, especially if the DM is conservative in handing out treasure. For example, }{\i\fs24 find familiar }{\fs24 
requires at least 1,000 gold pieces of special herbs and incenses, which means that a 1st\_level wizard may have to do a lot of adventuring before he has enough money to
 summon his familiar! The spell scare requires a piece of bone from an undead creature; requiring the wizard character to personally locate and remove such materials can force the player to make hard decisions about which spells are worth the trouble.

\par \tab Now
, imagine a game setting in which magic is far more costly. What if a character risked insanity every time he attempted to learn a spell? Or if the casting of a spell required the character to make a saving throw vs. spell or pass out from exhaustion? A c
h
aracter might even have to risk a permanent loss of hit points or ability scores each time he cast a spell in a world where magic is exceptionally dangerous. (See Chapter 6 for some of these options.) Again, these restrictions are rules changes and not ju
st cosmetic matters, but a few changes like these can make a great impact on a normally routine campaign.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Creating a World-View of Magic}{\fs24 
\par \tab How can the DM put all this together? Let\rquote s consider a couple of the AD&D campaign settings as examples. First of all, take a look at Faerun, the setting of the Forgotten Realms\'ae
 campaign. Magic is quite common in the Realms, and only slightly mysterious; everyone knows of the great wizards and the typical powers a wizard is likely to command, but there are a number of un
ique spells and magical items to be found. Magic is also fairly powerful in Faerun and comes with little cost or sacrifice to any character who works hard enough. The magic of the Realms is about average for an AD&D campaign.
\par \tab The Dark Sun\'ae campaign has an entirely different approach to magic. Magic is still fairly common and mysterious, but it can be extremely powerful (the sorcerer\_kings of Athas command 10th\_level magic) and comes at a great cost\emdash 
the defiling of any living vegetation nearby when a wizard casts a spell. In fact, the destruction caused by Athas\rquote  wizards is the chief cause of the planet\rquote s dessication and the rise of bizarre, mutated monsters.
\par \tab Last but not least, the Birthright\'99 campaign setting 
\par portrays a world in which wizard magic is rare. Mages are mysterious figures with unusual powers. Only a handful of characters have the heritage required to make use of true magic, and fewer still can command the kingdom\_shaking powers of realm magic.

\par \tab Here are a few ideas for alternative magic settings for your own campaign:
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 The College of Sorcerers: }{\fs24 In this setting, all wizards belong to a single guild or society cloaked in rite and mystery. (The imagers of Stephen R. Donaldson\rquote s }{\i\fs24 The Mirror of Her Dreams }{\fs24 
are a good example of such a society.) Spells may require a rare or unusual ingredient controlled by the College, or the College may treat spells as secrets that must be kept at any cost.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Secrets Man Was Not Meant to Know: }{\fs24 Wizard magic is the province of horrible pre\_human powers of the Outer Void,
 and dealing with them is the worst kind of betrayal. Insanity plagues those foolish enough to delve into the secrets of these elder powers; H.P. Lovecraft\rquote 
s stories are an excellent model of this kind of campaign. Chapter 6 describes a spell point system of magic that reflects this type of setting.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Smoke and Mirrors:}{\fs24  Wizards are far less powerful than they appear to be\emdash most are nothing more than alchemists and scholars who can command a few feeble spells. Any spell that creates something out of nothing or
 summons energy where no energy existed before cannot be cast; illusions, divinations, and minor alterations and summonings are the only types of magic that work. Many magical effects are accomplished through nonmagical means; for example, a }{\i\fs24 
pyrotechnics}{\fs24  spell is nothing more than a handful of chemical powder thrown on a flame.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 The Magical Renaissance:}{\fs24  In this world, almost everyone has a magical talent or two. Magic is fully integrated into society, not as a replacement for technology, but as a part of th
e common awareness and an augmentation of a person\rquote s skills. Chambermaids use }{\i\fs24 cantrips}{\fs24  to dust and to make beds, royal investigators have access to }{\i\fs24 speak with dead }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 ESP}{\fs24 
 to enforce the law, and many brilliant works of art are at least partly magical in nature. Even nonwizards may have a small selection of spells in this kind of setting.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 The Lost Powers: }{\fs24 Priests of this campaign have almost no spell powers. The various deities of their pantheon have lost the ability to grant spells to their followers or h
ave denied their followers spells for some reason. For a particularly chilling campaign, combine this thesis with the }{\b\fs24 Secrets Man Was Not Meant to Know }{\fs24 
scenario to create a world in which the only spellcasters with any power are the insane servants of inhuman powers.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Magic and Story Telling  }{\fs24 
When it comes down to it, an AD&D adventure is nothing more than a story created by the DM and embellished upon by the players. Every campaign generates volumes of epic confrontations, cliffhangers, and sinister villains\emdash it\rquote 
s just the way the game is played. Magic is often the central feature of these campaign stories; most players couldn\rquote t tell you a thing about Joe\rquote s paladin, but everyone who was at the game remembers the time Joe\rquote s paladin found the }
{\i\fs24 holy avenger}{\fs24 ! An 
enormous number of player characters are remembered fondly not for their personalities or the skill with which they were played, but instead the particular magical items they owned and the nifty tricks they had with their spell selections.
\par \tab While this isn\rquote t necessarily bad, it can detract from the role\_playing elements of a game if the players and the DM allow it to. After all, when the only tool you have is a hammer, everything starts looking like a nail\emdash and if you
\rquote ve got a }{\i\fs24 flame tongue }{\fs24 sword, it looks like the answer to any problem is going to involve slashing and burning }{\i\fs24 something}{\fs24 . Similarly, a wizard whose most memorable achievement is his invention of }{\i\fs24 Alkair
\rquote s inescapable decapitation }{\fs24 and subsequent use of the spell in every encounter for the rest of the campaign isn\rquote t really a well\_developed character at all. A hero with a magical sword is fine, but when the magical sword }{\i\fs24 
defines }{\fs24 the hero, the hero is diminished.
\par \tab Another difficulty that arises in many campaigns is the substitution of magic for technolo
gy. AD&D game players are (quite naturally) creatures of the modern world, and  modern conveniences such as automobiles, tanks, telephones, televisions, computers, and any number of other devices seem so indispensable that there just }{\i\fs24 have }{
\fs24 to be magical equivalents. While some of this is fine in any campaign, it desensitizes both players and DMs to the sheer wonder that magic should inspire in most characters. After all, magic should be }{\i\fs24 magical}{\fs24 
, full of mystery and terror, but when a character routinely uses magic to brush his teeth and stir his stew, magic becomes nothing more than a tool. Blurring the distinction between magic and technology detracts from the strength of both philosophies.

\par \tab Last, but not least, it\rquote s important to remember that magic can do}{\i\fs24  anything }{\fs24 that a DM needs it to do for purposes of advancing the plot or elaborating on an adventure. If the story calls for a greater tanar\rquote 
ri to be encased in a glass globe, it\rquote s not necessary to worry about exactly how the tanar\rquote ri was imprisoned there, or what spells the old archmage used to defeat the creature; it\rquote 
s okay for a DM to simply tell the players that the archmage did it. However, PCs and NPCs who are interacting with the party should follow the rules\emdash up to the point that the rules interfere with the story.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs36\cf6 Chapter 1:
\par Wizards
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 
The wizard may well be the most important character class in the AD&D game. Whether or not a particular player character wizard is the most powerful member of a party, it seems that }{\i\fs24 every }{\fs24 
AD&D campaign has at least one great archmage or master wizard who holds supreme power. Wizards are responsible for the creation of all kinds of adventures and works of magic that other characters later become entangled in or discover. In many adventures,
 the party\rquote s wizard is the }{\i\fs24 only }{\fs24 
character who can use his magic to provide a means to cross a barrier or solve a riddle. In fact, in some cases, the only way to resolve the entire adventure or quest is through the inventive use of the wizard\rquote s spell arsenal.
\par \tab Given this fact, it only makes sense to begin an examination of magic in the AD&D game with a thorough look at the wizard character class. In this chapter, we\rquote 
ll examine all the varieties of mages and specialist wizards available to a player character. Several new varieties of specialist wizards are also described in this chapter. In addition to a long look at the existing wizard classes, we\rquote 
ll also present a point\_based character design system that will allow a player to select his wizard\rquote s abilities and limitations in order to customize his own character. This system is an expansion of the character class rules from }{\i\fs24 Player
\rquote s Option: Skills & Powers}{\fs24 , although you don\rquote t need that book in order to use this material.
\par 
\par \tab The information in }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Spells & Magic }{\fs24 replaces or revises the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook,  The Complete Wizard\rquote s Handbook, the Tome of Magic}{\fs24 , and the material on magic from }{\i\fs24 Player
\rquote s Option: Skills & Powers}{\fs24 . In other words, if you have this book, you should use the rules presented here when creating your wizard character. However, there is one notable exception to this case\emdash 
if you are using an AD&D campaign setting that includes its own rules on character generation, such as the Dark Sun or Al\_Qadim\'ae game settings, you should continue to create wizards for those settings using the appropriate rules.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Spells from Other Sources}{\fs24 
\par \tab While the material on creating wizard characters is updated for this book, you\rquote ll find that spells that appeared in previous books have not been altered. As long as the
 DM approves, a player character wizard can learn spells from any source the player has available. In fact, the spell lists contained in Appendix 3 of this book include spells from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook, Tome of Magic}{\fs24 , and }{
\i\fs24 The Complete Wizard\rquote s Handbook }{\fs24 as well as dozens of new spells introduced in this book. Additional spells from the }{\i\fs24 Wizard\rquote s Spell Compendium, Pages from the Mages}{\fs24 
, or any other source can be approved by the DM on a case\_by\_case basis.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Schools of Magic  }{\fs24 All wizard spells belong to one or
 more schools of magic. A school of magic represents related spells with common features or characteristics. For a mage, who is the basic or general wizard, the school of a spell doesn\rquote 
t matter too much; he can learn and cast any spell without regard to the spell\rquote s school. The only exception to this rule is wild magic, which is completely unfathomable to any wizard except a wild mage.
\par \tab While the majority of wizards are mages, a significant number choose to be specialists who concentrate their efforts in one particular school. Generally, this increases the wizard\rquote 
s abilities within the school of his choice at the cost of losing access to any schools with opposing philosophies.
\par \tab There are three schemes of school organization used in the AD&D game: philosophy, effect, and thaumaturgy.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Schools of Philosophy}{\fs24 
\par \tab The eight standard schools of spells presented in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 \emdash abjuration, alteration, conjuration/summoning, enchantment/charm, greater divination, illusion/phantasm, invocation/evocation, and necromancy
\emdash 
are schools of philosophy. While all spells in this scheme of organization are cast in much the same way, the approach and method by which they achieve their purpose varies from school to school. For example, conjuration spells generally bring somet
hing to the caster from another location, while necromancy spells manipulate the forces of life and death.
\par \tab While spells in a school of philosophy generally involve the application of a common principle, they vary greatly in effect. For example, invocations create anything from solid matter such as }{\i\fs24 walls of stone }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 iron }{
\fs24 to comprehensive enchantments such as }{\i\fs24 contingency }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 limited wish}{\fs24 . Note that all spells grouped into schools of philosophy share the same execution or method of casting\emdash the use of verbal, s
omatic, and material components to summon and direct magical energy. The basic philosophies behind each school are briefly described below:
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Abjuration }{\fs24 spells are specialized protective spells designed to banish some magical or nonmagical effect or creature. }{\i\fs24 Protection from evil }{\fs24 
is an example of an abjuration spell, since it creates a barrier that evil or supernatural creatures are reluctant to cross.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Alteration }{\fs24 spells cause a change in the properties of some previously existing thing, creature, or condition. }{\i\fs24 Pyrotechnics }{\fs24 
is an alteration spell, since it takes an existing fire and creates special effects from the blaze.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Conjuration/Summoning }{\fs24 spells bring some intact item or creature to the caster from elsewhere. Any }{\i\fs24 monster summoning }{\fs24 spell is a good example.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Enchantment/Charm }{\fs24 spells cause a change in the quality of an item or the attitude of a person or creature. }{\i\fs24 Charm person }{\fs24 is an enchantment, since it affects the way an individual perceives the wizard.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Divinations }{\fs24 are spells that provide the wizard with information or the ability to acquire information.}{\i\fs24  Contact other plane }{\fs24 is a divination, since it allows the wizard to seek answers from extraplanar ent
ities. Note that this school has been somewhat altered in scope for this book; see }{\b\fs24 The School of Universal Magic}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Illusion/Phantasm }{\fs24 spells seek to deceive the minds or senses of others with false or semi\_substantial images and effects. }{\i\fs24 Phantasmal force }{\fs24 is a good example, as well as spells such as }{\i\fs24 
mirror image, invisibility, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 blur}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Invocation/Evocation }{\fs24 spells channel magical energy to create specific effects and materials. For example,}{\i\fs24  lightning bolt }{\fs24 manifests this energy in the form of a powerful stream of electricity.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Necromancy}{\fs24  is a school concerned with the manipulation of the forces of life and death. Necromancy spells include those that simulate the effects of undead creatures, such as }{\i\fs24 vampiric touch}{\fs24 
, and more direct assaults on life energy like }{\i\fs24 death spell }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 finger of death}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Universal Magic: }{\fs24 In this book, the school of lesser divination is expanded and renamed to include a number of spells that all wizards should have access to. Consider universal magic to be Sorcery 101; without the basic spells
 in this school, wizards are incapable of continuing their studies in the other schools of magic. Therefore, all wizards have access to the spells in this school, regardless of specialization. The school of universal magic is described in more detail belo
w.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Creating a New School of Philosophy: }{\fs24 
In most campaigns, the schools of philosophy represent the baseline or standard against which other forms of magic are measured. Almost all spells can be described through this system of magic, with very few exceptio
ns. For a wizard to develop a new school of philosophy, he would have to devise a class of spells that all share a common approach or methodology. Most likely, a group of related spells that already exist in one school or another would have to be used as 
the starting point for a new school. It\rquote s much easier to build a new school of effect or thaumaturgy than to build a new school of philosophy. }{\i\fs24 The Complete Wizard\rquote s Handbook }{\fs24 
suggests a school of transmutation that concentrates on spells that change one element or material into one other element or material. Another possibility might be a school of animation, centering on spells that provide motive force to inanimate objects.

\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Schools of Effect}{\fs24 
\par \tab A second scheme for organizing wizard magic is by effect. }{\i\fs24 The Tome of Magic }{\fs24 presented a new type of wizard who could specialize in schools of effect: the elementalist wizard. }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Skills & Powers }{
\fs24 introduced the shadow mage, another specialist wizard built around a school of effect.
\par \tab Schools of effect differ
 from schools of philosophy in that the spells of the school all share one common result or ingredient. For example, the spells of the school of fire all involve fire in some way, without regard for what the spell accomplishes. Divinations, the direction 
and control of energy, and the summoning of elementals can all be linked by the common effect of fire. The schools of effect are described below:
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Air: }{\fs24 The elemental school of air naturally includes any air\_based spell or effect, including spells that control or affect wind, breathing, falling and flight, air elementals, and other elemental phenomena. }{\i\fs24 Gust of wind }
{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 cloudkill }{\fs24 are examples of air spells.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Earth: }{\fs24 Spells of elemental earth are based around stone\_, earth\_, or mineral\_based effects. }{\i\fs24 Dig, stone shape }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 stone to flesh }{\fs24 are earth spells.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Fire: }{\fs24 Any spell involving the manifestation of flame or heat is a spell of elemental fire. Not all fire spells are attack spells; }{\i\fs24 fire charm }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 affect normal fires }{\fs24 
are examples of fire spells that don\rquote t cause direct and immediate damage to the wizard\rquote s enemies.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Water: }{\fs24 Last but not least in the elemental schools, spells of the school of elemental water involve water in some form or another, including spells of ice and cold, since these are linked to the element of water. }{\i\fs24 
Water breathing, ice storm}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 part water }{\fs24 are all included in the school of elemental water.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Dimensional Magic: }{\fs24 This is a new school introduced in }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Spells & Magic. }{\fs24 While all AD&D spells draw power from outside the mage, dimensionalists go one step further\emdash 
they draw their power from another dimension. Like most schools of effect, the school of dimensional magic overlaps several pre\_existing schools, including alteration, conjuration/summoning, and invocation spells.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Force: }{\fs24 The school of force is a new school of effect presented later in this book. A force mage relies on spells that create or manipulate fields of cohesive energy, such as }{\i\fs24 wall of force, magic missile, }{\fs24 
or any of the various }{\i\fs24 Bigby\rquote s hand }{\fs24 spells. Many spells of this school are borrowed from the school of invocation/evocation.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Shadow: }{\fs24 All the spells in this school are linked by the common effects of shadow and darkness. The shadow mage can make use of a number of illusion spells dealing with the Demiplane of Shadow and shadowstuff, including }{\i\fs24 
shadow monsters, darkness 15\rquote  radius}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 shadow walk}{\fs24 . He also has access to a number of necromancy spells. Although necromancy and illusion are opposing philosophies, schools of effect
 ignore these restrictions and concentrate on results.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Creating a School of Effect: }{\fs24 Again, all spells in a school of effect are cast with the standard execution of somatic, verbal, and material components. Creating a new school of effect is far easier tha
n coming up with a new school of philosophy; there are any number of common spell results or special effects that can be linked in this way. For example, a school of light could be designed around spells that produce bright visible effects, or a school of
 circles could be designed around any spell effect that is circular or spherical in form.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Schools of Thaumaturgy}{\fs24 
\par \tab A school of thaumaturgy defines a specific method or procedure of spellcasting that varies from the standard execution of a spell\rquote s components. Several schools of thaumaturgy were presented in }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Skills & Powers}
{\fs24 \emdash the school of song, the school of alchemy, and the school of geometry. In addition, other schools of thaumaturgy have appeared in specific campaign settings. The defiler of
 the Dark Sun campaign is a mage whose spells are executed through the draining of life energy from his surroundings. The sha\rquote ir of the Al\_
Qadim setting casts his spells by sending a servant gen, or minor genie, to fetch the spell and bring it back to him.
\par \tab Spells belonging to a school of thaumaturgy are not linked by philosophy or effect\emdash instead, they\rquote re related by the manner in which they are physically cast. In fact, the \ldblquote standard\rdblquote 
 approach to wizard magic defined by the eight philosophical schools represents one common thaumaturgical method. Other thaumaturgical methods include the following schools:
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 The School of Alchemy: }{\fs24 In this approach to magic, spell effects are achieved through the combination of unusual material components. Spells such as affect }{\i\fs24 normal fires, glitterdust, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 cloudkill }{\fs24 
can all be cast through the use of strange powders and reagents, and belong to the school of alchemy.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 The School of Artifice: }{\fs24 This is a new school introduced here for the first time. Artificers are weak in the direct command of magic and instead use various devices and magical items to focus their energies. Spells such as }{\i\fs24 
Melf\rquote s minute meteors }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 magic staff }{\fs24 are included in the school of artifice.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 The School of Geometry: }{\fs24 
Geometers use diagrams, symbols, and complex patterns to cast their spells. Naturally, any spell involving some kind of writing, marking, or pattern belongs to the school of geometry, including spells such as }{\i\fs24 explosive runes, sepia snake sigil, 
}{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 symbol}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 The School of Song: }{\fs24 While alchemists rely on material components and geometers rely on somatic components, a song mage uses the power of his voice to summon and shape spell energy. Any spell that involves speaking, singing
, or some other use of the caster\rquote s vocal powers belongs to the school of song. }{\i\fs24 Sleep, charm monster, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 Otto\rquote s irresistible dance }{\fs24 are all examples of spells of this school.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 The School of Wild Magic: }{\fs24 Some wizards have learned to make use of the principle
s of randomness in their magic, giving rise to the school of wild magic. Wild mages shape the raw, uncontrollable stuff of magic in the hope that something resembling their intended spell will appear. A number of wild magic spells such as }{\i\fs24 
vortex }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 waveform }{\fs24 appeared in the }{\i\fs24 Tome of Magic}{\fs24 ; if you do not have access to that book, you should probably ignore this school.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 The School of Universal Magic}{\fs24 
\par \tab As described in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 , the school of divination is actually composed of lesser divinations and
 greater divinations. A few specialist mages are barred from greater divination as an opposition school, but every specialist wizard is considered to have access to lesser divination. In this book, the school of lesser divination is replaced by the school
 of universal magic. This includes a few basic divination and nondivination spells that all wizards should have access to, such as }{\i\fs24 dispel magic }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 
. Any wizard may cast spells of this school, regardless of his or her specialty.
\par \tab The divination spells of both the schools of lesser and greater divination are now considered to be part of one school of divination. Spells such as }{\i\fs24 ESP, clairaudience, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance }{\fs24 
are part of the school of divination and may not be available to wizards who formerly had access to them as lesser divinations. Refer to Appendix 3 for the revised spell organization.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Important Note: }{\fs24 Wizards do not automatically know universal spells. They must study and attempt to learn the spells of this school, just like any other s
pells. However, when a wizard character is first created, he automatically begins play with any 1st\_level universal spells of his choice in his spell book, although these count against the character\rquote s limit of beginning spells.
\par \tab The school of universal magic consists of the following spells:
