\par \tab Most of the low\_level spells on this list enable the wizard to undertake his basic studies in books of arcane lore and safeguard his laboratory and spell book against intruders. }{\i\fs24 Teleport, teleport without error}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 
astral spell }{\fs24 are included since the ability to travel vast distances in the blink of an eye is a common power among wizards in fantasy literature. Last but not least, }{\i\fs24 enchant an item }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 
are universal magic because every wizard should have the ability to create magical items when he or she reaches the appropriate level.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Wizard Characters  }{\fs24 A player creating a wizard character has one basic decision to make when the character is first rolled up\emdash 
 should his character specialize in a school of magic, or should he remain a mage? A mage is equally capable
 in all schools of magic and may freely learn and cast spells from any school. A specialist wizard gains several important benefits when dealing with his own school, but loses access to certain spells that belong to opposition schools.
\par \tab As noted in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
, wizards may not wear armor and are limited in their selection of weapons to the dagger, dart, knife, sling, and staff. (Some character kits may allow additional weapon choices.) In addition, characters constructed with the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote 
s Option: Skills & Powers }{\fs24 rules or the expanded character point rules in this book may pay extra character points in order to gain access to better weapons or protective equipment.
\par \tab All wizards may create magical potions or scrolls after reaching 9th level by using 
the magical item creation rules in this book. (The alchemist and geometer gain this ability earlier in their careers.) Wizards may also create other types of magical items upon reaching 11th level. Any wizard may attempt to research new spells, regardless
 of level.
\par }{\fs28\cf6 
\par The Wizard\rquote s Spell Book}{\fs24 
\par \tab A 1st\_level wizard begins play with 3d4 1st\_level spells in his spell book, two of which must be}{\i\fs24  read magic }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24 
. Once these two have been included in the spell book, the player may select any other 1st\_level
 spells of the school of universal magic without making a learn spells roll. In addition, a specialist wizard may automatically choose one spell of his specialty to begin play with. Beyond these selections, the player must attempt a learn spells check for
 any additional spells he wishes his character to know, with the normal penalties or bonuses for specialization. Optionally, the DM may assign a beginning wizard character }{\i\fs24 read magic, detect magic}{\fs24 , and four other spells of the DM\rquote 
s choice.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Adding Spells to the Wizard\rquote s Repertoire: }{\fs24 
As a wizard continues with his adventuring career, he will encounter new spells that he may wish to add to his spell book. In addition, mages may add a spell to their book whenever they reach a new spell level, while specialist wi
zards are allowed to add one spell of their specialty to their spell books each time they gain an experience level. Last but not least, the DM may allow a PC wizard to purchase spells from an NPC wizard or organization. The price should be a spell of equa
l level that the NPC doesn\rquote t know, a magical item other than a potion or scroll, or at least 1,000 gp per level of the spell in question.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Mage}{\fs24 
\par Ability Requirements: Intelligence 9
\par Prime Requisite: Intelligence
\par Races Allowed: Human, Elf, Half\_elf
\par 
\par \tab The mage remains largely unchanged by the material presented in }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Skills & Powers}{\fs24 
. A mage may learn and cast spells of any school (except wild magic) using the normal wizard spell progression table and learn spells rolls. Naturally, mages may make use of many of the new spells and magical items introduced in this book.
\par \tab Mages with an Intelligence score of 16 or higher gain a 10% bonus to the experience points they earn. Mages never attract followers, but there\rquote s no reason a mage couldn\rquote t buy property
 and hire mercenaries whenever he accumulates sufficient wealth.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Specialist Wizard}{\fs24 
\par Ability Requirements: Varies
\par Prime Requisite: Intelligence
\par Races Allowed: Varies
\par 
\par \tab Wizards who concentrate their efforts in one school of magic are known as specialist wizards. Generally, a specialist wizard must give up some degree of versatility in spell selection\emdash 
he cannot learn or cast spells belonging to schools that oppose his own chosen school. The specialist also has several other benefits and restrictions; unless otherwise stated, all specialists must abide by the benefits and hindrances described below:

\par 
\par  \tab Specialist wizards may memorize one additional spell per spell level, provided the spell selected belongs to the specialist\rquote s school. Under this rule, a 1st\_level specialist may have two spells memorized instead of only one.
\par   \tab Specialists gain a bonus of +1 when making saving throws against spells of their own school. Specialists also inflict a \endash 1 penalty to their victims\rquote  saving throw attempts when casting a spell of their specialty school.
\par  \tab Specialist wizards gain a bonus of +15% when learning spells from their school, but suffer a penalty of \endash 15% when learning spells from any other school. Specialists cannot learn spells belonging to an opposition school.
\par  \tab When a specialist reaches a new level, he automatically gains one spell of his school to add to his spell book. No roll for learning the spell need be made.
\par  \tab When a specialist wizard attempts to create a new spell through research, the spell is treated as if it were one level lower if it falls within the wizard\rquote s specialty school.
