\par \tab Secondly, the wizard may memorize one casting of his signature spell at no cost in spells available at that level. In other words, the character gains the specialist wizard benefit of memorizing additional spells. For example, a 1st\_
level mage may normally memorize one 1st\_level spell. If the mage has }{\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 as a signature spell, he may memorize one 1st\_level spell, plus an additional }{\i\fs24 magic missile}{\fs24 , for a total of two 1st\_
level spells. If the character is a specialist invoker, he can memorize three 1st\_level spells: }{\i\fs24 magic missile}{\fs24 , a second invocation spell, and the 1st\_level spell he normally receives as a 1st\_level wizard. N
ote that the character in this example could choose to spend his discretionary spells to memorize a total of three }{\i\fs24 magic missiles}{\fs24 , which wouldn\rquote t be a bad move considering that he\rquote s so good at casting it!
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Role-Playing Signature Spells: }{\fs24 A wizard with a signature spell often becomes notorious for his use of the spell. Everyone recalls stories of the transmuter who }{\i\fs24 polymorphed }{\fs24 
folks into frogs, or the fire mage who threw }{\i\fs24 fireballs }{\fs24 left and right. The DM should encourage the player to develop his character\rquote s motivations for becoming so skilled with one particular enchantment.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Priest Proficiencies  }{\fs24 Like wizards, priests acquire a variety of nonweapon proficiencies over the course of their careers. More than any other character, a priest is likely to find a use 
for common or everyday skills. In fact, in many cultures, priests are the principal teachers, scholars, or craftsmen of their societies. Priests have a nonweapon proficiency progression that equals that of a wizard, but gain weapon skills much faster.

\par \tab In this section, we\rquote ll take a look at some new priest proficiencies. These should be considered addenda to the Table 37: Nonweapon Proficiency Groups in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
. Refer to Table 10: New Priest Nonweapon Proficiencies.
