\par }{\fs28\cf6 Cantrips
\par }{\fs24 \tab The }{\i\fs24 cantrip }{\fs24 spell from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook }{\fs24 is intended to describe a host of minor magical effects. In this system, a wizard may choose to memorize a }{\i\fs24 cantrip }{\fs24 as a 1st\_level spell
\emdash just as it\rquote s described in the }{\i\fs24 PHB}{\fs24 \emdash or he can memorize individual applications of }{\i\fs24 cantrip }{\fs24 for a cost of 1 spell point each. These one\_shot cantrips have the exact same effect as the 1st\_
level spell, but each application consumes 1 SP and has a duration of one full turn.
\par \tab All }{\i\fs24 cantrips}{\fs24  are free magicks by definition; the wizard can perform any minor magical feat appropriate for the spell, without selecting the precise incantation ahead of time. A wizard is limited in the number of }{\i\fs24 cantrips }
{\fs24 he can memorize at one time; he can\rquote t allocate more than twice his number of spells allowed for each level to }{\i\fs24 cantrips}{\fs24 . For example, a 1st\_
level mage is limited to no more than two spells of each level, so he can memorize a maximum of four }{\i\fs24 cantrips}{\fs24 . A 6th\_level mage is limited to four spells of any given level, so he can memorize up to eight }{\i\fs24 cantrips}{\fs24 .

\par \tab All normal considerations for cantrips as described in the }{\i\fs24 PHB }{\fs24 still apply\emdash they are minor magic, useful in role\_
playing or grandstanding but generally useless in combat. Of course, clever and resourceful players can create plenty of mayhem, even with magic as weak as this.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Exceeding the Spell Level Limit}{\fs24 
\par \tab If the DM agrees, a wizard character may learn and cast spells of a higher level than he would normally be allowed to know. This blurs the line of spell level distinctions, making the character\rquote 
s exact level somewhat less important; there will be spells a character can learn and use easily, as well as spells that will be extremely costly and difficult for the character to use.
\par \tab Before a character can memorize a spell that\rquote s over his head, he h
as to learn it first. This means that the character must find, purchase, or otherwise stumble across a spell of the level in question and attempt a learn spells check to see if he can master it. However, when a character is dabbling in matters beyond his 
depth, his learn spells chance of success is }{\i\fs24 halved }{\fs24 for a spell one level higher than he should be able to use, and divided by }{\i\fs24 10 }{\fs24 
for a spell two levels higher than the maximum normally allowed. If a character does not succeed in learning the spell at this point, then he can try again at the level that he normally gains the spell. Under no circumstances can a character learn a spell
 three levels higher than his normal maximum\emdash it\rquote s just too difficult, and he\rquote 
ll have to study his art for a while longer before he can make heads or tails of it. Also, because the spell is so difficult to learn, the wizard must memorize it as a fixed magick.
\par \tab If the character succeeds in learning the high\_level spell, he can then select it normally by using his spell points to memorize it. However, since the spell is extraordinarily difficult for the character, he must pay }{\i\fs24 twice }{\fs24 
the listed normal fixed magick cost to memorize it. For many low\_level characters, this will make the spell prohibitively expensive in any event; a 2nd\_
level specialist wizard only has a total of 12 spell points available, so he could just barely memorize a single 2nd\_level spell of his specialty school. Note that a 2nd\_level mage just doesn\rquote t have the points available to even try this feat.

\par \tab Wizards of medium to high level have a better chance to use a spell that would normally be out of reach for them. For example, a 6th\_level wizard normally is limited to 3rd\_level spells, but with effort he can learn a 4th\_
level spell and cast it for 30 spell points, which is twice the normal cost of 15 spell points. Since a 6th\_level mage has 55 spell points available, he actually has a few points left over to select other spells\emdash although it\rquote 
s a very costly privilege!
\par \tab Again, this is an optional rule, and the DM can decide to ignore it for his campaign. Note that all level\_based characteristics of a spell depend on the caster\rquote s true level, so even if a 3rd\_level wizard somehow acquired a }{\i\fs24 fireball
}{\fs24 , it would only do 3d6 damage. (And he wouldn\rquote t have the points available to memorize it, anyway.)
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Casting Spells For Greater Effect}{\fs24 
\par \tab Another variant rule available with the spell point system is casting spells for greater than normal effect. By \ldblquote overcharging\rdblquote 
 a memorized spell (fixed magick only) with magical energy, a wizard can achieve results as if he were a wizard of much higher level. For example, a 2nd\_level wizard is normally limited to a single }{\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 
when he casts the spell, but with this optional rule he can fire the spell as a 3rd\_level character in order to create two missiles with the spell. Naturally, the wizard must devote additional spell points to the spell when he memorizes it. The pr
ice is 50% of the spell\rquote s base cost for each additional casting level stored with the spell.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Rarik is a 4th\_level mage who wants to gain the extra damage potential of a third }{\fs24 magic missile }{\i\fs24 
when he casts the spell. Normally, a third missile is gained at 5th level, so Rarik wants to cast the spell as if he were one level higher. A 1st\_
level spell requires 4 spell points to memorize, but by spending 6 points (50% more than 4), Rarik gains the ability to cast this one spell as if he were actually 5th level. All level\_
based variables are affected, including range, area of effect, duration, saving throw modifiers, and any other appropriate factors, so Rarik incidentally gains an additional 10 yards of range with his investment.
\par \tab Later on in his career, Rarik decides that he\rquote d like a }{\fs24 fireball }{\i\fs24 with some extra punch. He\rquote s a 5th\_
level mage now, and the spell costs him 10 spell points. Rarik spends two 50% increments (a grand total of 20 spell points) to cast this fireball as if he were a 7th\_
level caster. The range increases from 60 to 80 yards, and the damage is 7d6 instead of 5d6.
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab A character can\rquote t invest more than four additional levels into a spell, which would triple its cost, increasing it by 200% above the normal price. Most wizards find that it\rquote 
s more efficient to gain extra spells rather than overcharge a single one, but for spells with important breaks in power level based on the caster\rquote s level\emdash like }{\i\fs24 magic missile}{\fs24 \emdash one or two steps of increased 
casting level may be a good idea.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Reducing Spell Cost}{\fs24 
\par \tab Just as wizards can allocate extra energy to hold magicks free, cast spells of higher level than normal, or gain additional levels of casting ability, they may also save spell points by memorizing fix
ed magicks with limitations or reduced power levels. A spell may be reduced in cost by 25% (round up) per limitation selected, to a maximum reduction of 50% for two limitations. Restrictions include the following:
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Reduced Spell Power: }{\fs24 By investing a spell with less energy than normal, a wizard can save a few spell points. This reduces the caster\rquote s level by four, affecting range, duration, damage, and all other level\_
based variables. In addition, the spell can be more easily }{\i\fs24 dispelled}{\fs24 , since the effective casting level is used for this purpose.
\par \tab If the spell has}{\i\fs24  no }{\fs24 level\_based variables (e.g., }{\i\fs24 charm person}{\fs24 ) this limitation cannot be selected. A wizard must be at least 5th level in order to take this limitation.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Prolonged Casting Time: }{\fs24 The wizard can store l
ess energy than normal in the spell, hoping to draw energy from his immediate environment when the time comes to cast it. This results in a lengthy period of gathering energy before actually releasing the spell. Spells with a casting time of less than one
 round require a preparation time of a number of rounds equal to the spell\rquote 
s listed casting time, so a spell with a casting time of 3 would require three full rounds of uninterrupted concentration before the wizard could actually cast the spell. Spells with
 a casting time of one round require one full turn to cast, and spells with a casting time of more than one round can\rquote t be reduced in cost through this limitation.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Special Casting Condition: }{\fs24 By narrowing the focus and application of a spell, a wizard can 
reduce the amount of energy and effort required to commit it to memory. For example, the wizard can memorize a spell that can only be cast by the light of the moon or only affects certain types of objects (such as a }{\i\fs24 shatter }{\fs24 
on crystal only). If the spell i
s particularly restrictive, the DM may allow a 50% reduction in cost. For example, a spell that can only be cast while the wizard is standing in one specific place of power in the middle of nowhere might qualify. Naturally, the DM should keep a }{\i\fs24 
very }{\fs24 close eye on spells with this limitation; if it\rquote s not really a limitation, the wizard shouldn\rquote t catch a break for it.
\par \tab Instead of requiring certain conditions to be right for casting, the wizard might have to take special steps to ensure that the target can be affected. For example, his }{\i\fs24 charm person }{\fs24 spell might require the caster to know the target
\rquote s name or spend a turn conversing with the subject. Or, a }{\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 might have no effect unless the wizard has touched the subject with his bare hand that day. It\rquote 
s even possible that a wizard may have a spell that automatically fails against a target wearing a certain color or carrying a lucky charm.
\par \tab A wizard shouldn\rquote t have more than one or two casting conditions that he normally observes. It\rquote s fine for a shadow mage to have a number of spells that can\rquote t be cast in daylight, but if he also has another spell that can\rquote 
t be used while it\rquote s raining, and a third that only affects orcs, the character concept is clearly being discarded in favor of game effect.
