\par }{\fs28\cf11 Channellers
\par }{\fs24 \tab Imagine wizards who do }{\i\fs24 not }{\fs24 forget spells as they are cast, but instead freely use any spell in their repertoire whenever they wish. Wizards in this system of magic are born with some special spar
k or gift that allows them to reach some hidden source or supply of magical energy and use this to power their enchantments and spells. A young apprentice knows only one or two ways to harness this energy into useful applications and has a very limited ca
pacity for tapping and shaping magical energy, while a great archmage knows dozens upon dozens of spells and has a nearly unlimited capability for wielding magical energy.
\par \tab While this may seem like a world where the wizards reign unchecked, there\rquote s an impor
tant catch: Reaching for and directing magical energy is a dangerous and taxing exercise, at least as difficult as heavy labor or prolonged exertion, and each spell a wizard casts leaves him weakened and vulnerable. In fact, wizards can exhaust their very
 lives by casting a spell that\rquote s too much for them to handle or by casting too many smaller spells in succession.
\par \tab Channelling or summoning magical energy is a very common system of magic in fantasy literature. Here\rquote s how it works in the Player\rquote s Option rul
es: Basically, the wizard gains spell points as described in the beginning of this chapter, allocates them to fixed magicks or free magicks just as he wishes, and selects the particular spells he wants to have locked into memory as fixed magicks. (Since t
he wizard takes the time to impress these spells in his mind, it\rquote s easier for him to energize them with channelled magic, and thus the spell point cost is lower than free magicks.) The following optional systems from the previous section are used:

\par 
\par  \tab Free magicks may be used to provide the wizard with access to any spell in his repertoire, although they\rquote re still more expensive than fixed magicks.
\par  \tab The wizard may exceed his normal spell level limit using the optional rules described previously.
\par  \tab The wizard may decide to cast spells for greater effect by simply spending more spell points when he actually casts the spell.
\par  \tab The wizard can save spell points by choosing to cast spells with a prolonged casting time or at reduced power.
\par  \tab Lastly, the wizard modifies his spell point total based on his Wisdom and Constitution scores; he may not gain bonus spell points for his Intelligence. The character\rquote 
s hit point adjustment for Constitution and his magical attack adjustment for Wisdom are added to or subtracted from his spell point total. If this lowers a 1st\_
level character to less than 4 spell points, he ignores the adjustments; all wizards have at least 4 spell points.
\par 
\par \tab While the character may have some spell points \ldblquote allocated\rdblquote  or \ldblquote tied up\rdblquote  in various fixed and 
free magicks, this actually makes no difference for a channeller. The initial selection of spells is simply used to create a slate of spell powers that the character can access and to define the cost in spell points for making use of these powers. The cha
racter may cast any spell that he has available through either a fixed or free magick, except that the magick does not vanish from his memory once he\rquote 
s cast the spell. Instead, the character deducts the number of spell points required to energize the spell 
from his spell point total. For example, if a mage with 40 spell points has a magic missile memorized, he can cast that magic missile four times if he wants to!
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf11 Channellers and Studying Spells: }{\fs24 Since spells never vanish from a channeller\rquote s memory, the character does not normally re\_
memorize spells during the course of an adventure. However, channellers have no particular obstacles to learning new spells or replacing the spells held as fixed or free magicks in their memory. The character must be well\_rested
 and have access to his spell books. It takes 10 minutes per spell level for the character to memorize a new spell. In effect, he \ldblquote overwrites\rdblquote 
 whichever spells he wishes to in order to change the allocation of spell points into free and fixed magicks.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Kerian, a 5th\_level channelling invoker, wishes to change the spells he has memorized. Because of his Constitution bonus, he has 61 points to assign to various magicks. After resting, he decides to memorize one fixed (}{\fs24 shield}{
\i\fs24 ) and one free 1st\_level magick (12 SPs), two fixed magicks (}{\fs24 web }{\i\fs24 and }{\fs24 invisibility }{\i\fs24 for 12 SPs), and one prolonged casting time 3rd\_level magick (}{\fs24 fireball}{\i\fs24  for 7 SPs), and one 4th\_
level magick that exceeds his level (}{\fs24 ice storm }{\i\fs24 at 30 SPs). After memorizing the spells, which takes 1 hour and 10 minutes, Kerian is ready to adventure!
\par }{\fs24 
\par If a character wishes to change just one spell in his current repertoire, he still has to rest and memorize it. For example, if a mage wished to change his }{\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 spell to a }{\i\fs24 shield }{\fs24 
spell, then he would have to sleep for eight hours and spend 10 minutes committing it to memory. 
\par \tab Since spell points in this system represent magic potential or stamina (for lack of better words), expended spell points are naturally recovered as the character\rquote 
s fatigue fades and his strength returns. Just as a human who runs a wind sprint eventually recovers from his temporary exhaustion, a wizard who casts a spell will soon return to his full magical strength with a little rest. Spell points are recovered
 as shown on Table 20: Spell Point Recovery for Channellers.
\par \tab Characters recover a number of spell points equal to the percentage listed, or the whole number, whichever is better. For example, a 6th\_level mage has 55 spell points. If he sleeps, 10% of 55 w
ould be 6 (round up 5.5) spell points per hour, so he recovers 8 per hour instead. In fact, it\rquote 
s more advantageous for any mage of 7th level or lower to take the listed number, and mages of 8th level or higher to work with percentages instead. For specialists, 7th\_level is the break point.
\par \tab In addition to the long\_term prospects of exhausting his spell points, a channelling wizard finds that each spell he casts temporarily tires him. See Table 21: Spell Fatigue.
