\par 
\par \tab In this system, the deity or power is concerned more with the priest\rquote s show of devotion and observance of the proper form, and considers the priest\rquote s actual situation to be irrelevant\emdash after all, martyrs
 are made every day. The priest purchases his initial selection of spells by allocating spell points to free and fixed theurgies of the various spell levels. These represent very specific prayers that he will use to focus his deity\rquote 
s power, if the power chooses to respond.
\par \tab The normal options and rules governing priestly spell points are in use. In addition, the priest may make use of the reduced spell cost option, choosing the prolonged casting time in exchange for a 25% reduction in the spell cost. This e
xtends the casting time of a spell to the next highest time increment, so a spell with a casting time of 4 would require four }{\i\fs24 rounds }{\fs24 to cast, and a spell with a casting time of a full round would take a turn to cast.
\par \tab Before the priest can cast a spell, he must first invoke his deity\rquote 
s attention by speaking various preparatory prayers and performing other actions favorable to his power. In game terms, he must accumulate enough spell points to power the desired spell. Low\_level spells are very easy to cast, 
requiring nothing more than a moment\rquote s concentration and a brief invocation. However, it can take a long time to build a higher\_
level spell. A ritual priest gathers 2 spell points per round through his invocations and prayers, but the cost to cast a spell is reduced by the priest\rquote s level. For example, a 1st\_level priest casting a 1st\_
level spell must gather 3 spell points (4 for the spell, less 1 for his level), which would require one full round of prayer, plus part of a second round.
\par \tab If the priest\rquote s level reduces the spell\rquote 
s cost to 0, the priest need not spend any time gathering spell points and casts the spell with the normal casting time initiative modifiers. In the example above, a priest of 4th level or higher reduces a 1st\_level spell to a cost of 0
, which means he can cast the spell without spending time to gather his deity\rquote s favor beforehand. In effect, the spell is minor enough that the power trusts the priest not to misuse his or her divine energy.
\par \tab In many cases, the priest\rquote s gathering time will require more than one round. The 1st\_
level priest described above must gather 3 spell points to cast his spell, which means that he gathers 2 points in the first round of concentration, and the remaining point in the round in which he casts the spell. The
re is a slight initiative modifier for this, as shown on Table 30: Initiative Modifiers for Ritual Prayer.
\par \tab The initiative modifier is based on the number of spell points accumulated }{\i\fs24 in the round in which the spell is cast}{\fs24 . For example, a 5th\_level priest casting a 3rd\_
level spell must draw 5 spell points (10 for the spell, less 5 for his level), which requires two full rounds of concentration and part of a third\emdash but since he only needs to gather 1 spell point in the last round, he gains a \endash 1 b
onus to his initiative when he actually casts the spell.
\par \tab There are ways to accelerate this otherwise tedious process. Invoking the deity\rquote s power in a sanctified place, making a sacrifice pleasing to the deity, or staying true to the deity\rquote s precepts are a
ll good ways to ensure that the deity in question will be willing to empower the priest with spell energy. See Table 31: Modifiers to Ritual Preparatory Times for examples.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Place/Site of Interest: }{\fs24 The priest is located in some place favored by the god. This could be a forge for a deity of smithery, a business for a god of commerce, or a battlefield for a power of war.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Sanctified or Holy Place: }{\fs24 The priest is located in a shrine, temple, or place of special and unique significance to the deity he serves.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Offerings: }{\fs24 The priest destroys or surrenders items or materials pleasing to his patron power. This could range from treasure, weapons, or gems to such things as exotic spices, candles, or specially\_
prepared libations. A deity of war or battle might consider a gem\_
decorated sword to be a minor offering, a sword of special significance or quality to be a moderate offering, and a magical weapon to be a major offering. As a rule of thumb, a small offering must be worth at least 50 gp, a moderate worth 500 gp, a
nd a major offering 2,000 gp or more.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Purity and Faithfulness: }{\fs24 This is a subjective call on the part of the DM. Has the priest been careful to follow the precepts of both his temple and his alignment? Has the player gone out of his way to role\_play his character
\rquote s dedication, or has he been lazy in portraying the priest? If the character has been played well and is in favor with his superiors and his deity, this bonus applies.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Previously Expended Spell: }{\fs24 While the ritual priest generally expends spells normally, it is possible for him to gather spell energy in order to re\_
use a spell that he already cast. However, this is quite difficult, and unless the priest has some other factor going for him\emdash an offering or a good location from which to cast\emdash he will be unable to attract the divine power necessary to re\_
use a spell.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Regaining Spell Points: }{\fs24 As noted above, ritual priests expend their spells normally, striking them from memory as they cast the enchantments. In order to ready himself to use a spell again or 
change his spell allocation, the priest must rest for at least eight hours and spend approximately 10 minutes per spell level praying for the spells he desires. Despite the fact that ritual priests gain spell energy directly from their deities, the compli
cated prayers and forms required to cast a spell must still be refreshed occasionally.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf9 Conditional Magic
\par }{\fs24 \tab In this system of priestly magic, deities grant the ability to cast spells based on the priest\rquote s need, not just on form or prayer. A faithful priest following his patron power\rquote 
s tenets will usually receive the spells he requests, but a priest who strays from the path will soon find that his patron no longer supports his actions. While the urgency of the priest\rquote s request is a consideration, the deity\rquote s p
rimary concern is whether or not the use of that particular spell will benefit the deity\rquote s own purposes and causes at that moment. The priest\rquote s spell powers will vary with the interest and support of his deity.
\par 
\par The following option is used for conditional magic:
\par 
\par \tab Spells belonging to a minor sphere of access are more costly than spells from a major sphere, as described earlier. Priests may request free or fixed theurgies by meeting the normal spell point costs.
\par \tab Many of the normal priest restrictions are still in place for priests using conditional magic. First of all, priests must still \ldblquote memorize\rdblquote 
 any spell that he wishes to have access to. After all, if the priest is granted the ability to cast the spell, then he should be familiar with exactly how to go 
about doing so! Secondly, while the priest can cast spells at a higher character level than his own, he cannot use spells from spell lists above his own spell level.
\par \tab Before a priest actually begins play using this system of magic, the player and the DM should take some time to define }{\i\fs24 positive }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 negative conditions }{\fs24 for that priest\rquote 
s faith. These are situations or guidelines that define the deity\rquote s interests and ensure that the priest only invokes his god\rquote s power in pursuit of the deity\rquote s aims. Here are some examples of conditions:
\par 
