\par +2\tab Caster may cast the spell as if he were 1 level higher (no additional SP cost) or 
\par \tab choose to reduce the spell point expenditure by 50%.
\par +1\tab No unusual effects.
\par   0\tab Caster must either select one negative effect of reduced spell cost and pay the 
\par \tab normal spell point cost, or he can cast the spell at the usual level by paying 150% 
\par \tab of the normal cost.
\par \endash 1\tab Caster must select two negative effects of reduced spell cost and pay the normal 
\par \tab spell point cost, or spend twice as many spell points as normal in order to cast the 
\par \tab spell without reduced effect.
\par \endash 2\tab The spell fails completely, expending the normal amount of spell points required.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Positive Conditions}{\fs24\cf9 
\par }{\fs24 \tab The priest is engaged in combat against a traditional enemy of the god or the faith, such as giants for a priest of Thor, or orcs for the priest of an elven god.
\par \tab The priest is located in a place sacred or special to the deity, such as a mountaintop for a god of the air, or a deep forest for a druidical power.
\par \tab The priest is engaged in a situation favorable to 
\par the deity; fighting for a god of war, trading for a merchant power, committing theft or burglary for a god of thieves, or in the open during a storm for a storm god.
\par \tab The priest\rquote s spell will directly benefit another worshipper of the same deity, such as healing a paladin of the same faith or using spells to protect townspeople who follow the priest\rquote s religion.
\par \tab The spell fulfills a traditional role or guideline of the power\emdash starting a fire for a fire god, sowing deceit for a god of mischief, or offering advice or guidance in the case of a god of wisdom or knowledge.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf9 Negative Conditions
\par }{\fs24 \tab The spell aids or supports a traditional enemy.
\par \tab The priest is located in a place inimical or opposed to his power (a wind priest adventuring underground, a fire priest venturing out on the ocean, etc.)
\par \tab The priest is involved in a situation distasteful or
 opposed to his patron power (a priest of chaos using his spells to restore order, a priest of healing attempting to injure someone with his spells, a priest of battle trying to avoid or escape from a fight).
\par \tab The spell will directly or indirectly injure or discomfit a fellow worshipper or the temple\rquote s interests.
\par \tab The priest has failed to observe one of his patron\rquote s holy days or rites, has violated his alignment, or has generally misrepresented his patron recently.
\par 
\par \tab As an example of how these conditions mi
ght work, consider the case of a priest of Tempus from the Forgotten Realms campaign setting. (Tempus is a chaotic deity of battle and storms.) For his positive conditions, he selects casting spells in battle, casting spells during a storm, and casting sp
ells that enhance or augment a warrior\rquote s ability to fight, such as }{\i\fs24 bless, emotion}{\fs24 
, and other such enchantments. For his negative conditions, he selects casting spells outside of a fight, avoiding or fleeing a battle, and spells that aid lawful characters. N
aturally, the DM can either set up these conditions himself or allow the player to set them 
\par up and then approve the conditions.
\par \tab Any time the priest casts a spell, the player should compare the number of positive conditions that apply to the number of negative conditions, and consult Table 32: Effects of Conditions. 
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Maglas, the priest of Tempus previously described, is locked in battle with an ogre during a thunderstorm. Deciding that things aren\rquote t going his way, he decides to cast an }{\fs24 obscurement }{\i\fs24 spell 
to cover his escape. Since he is in battle, and the weather is appropriate, he has two positive conditions. On the other hand, he\rquote 
s getting ready to flee, which is a negative condition. There is a net +1 positive condition, which means that Maglas can cast the spell normally.
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab In this system, if a priest casts a spell, it\rquote s still expended until he can re\_memorize it . . . but saving spell points may allow the character to find room for an extra free theurgy. For example, if Maglas manages to save 8 spell p
oints by casting several spells in favorable conditions, he may now add a 1st\_level free theurgy to his spell repertoire for the day.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf9 Recovering Spell Points: }{\fs24 Priests who use conditional magic recover their spell points normally (see }{\b\fs24 Recovering Spell Points}{\fs24 
) and may change their spell selection any time they rest eight hours and spend the time to pray for new spells.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf14 Druidical Magic}{\fs24\cf14 
\par }{\fs24 \tab Priests of druidical or agricultural powers may be able to use preserving magic (see }{\b\fs24 Defilers and Preservers}{\fs24 ). Preservers d
raw their magical energy from the lifeforce that surrounds them but are careful not to draw too much. If the preserving magic system is allowed for wizards, this option should not be available for priests\emdash it\rquote 
s a good idea to keep the flavor and methodology of wizard magic and priest magic distinct and separate. 
\par \tab Preservers strongly resemble ritual priests, since both must gather their energy before casting their spells, but preservers harness the lifeforce around them, while ritual priests rely on the power of their deity.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Other Spellcasters  }{\fs24 Wizards and priests aren\rquote 
t the only spellcasters in the AD&D game; paladins, rangers, and bards also have magical abilities. These are represented by spell point progression tables similar to those of the previous sections, although these characters don\rquote 
t acquire their spell powers until a little bit later in their careers.
\par 
