\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Cost and Time: }{\fs24 Again, the cost and time required to manufacture magical items varies depending on the category and the type of item. For example, if a mage wishes to create a single\_
function ring worth 1,000 XP, then he must not only spend 1,000 gp (see the \ldblquote Cost in XP\rdblquote 
 column of Table 38: Magical Item Cost and Time Requirements), but he must also spend 10 weeks (one week for every 100 gp spent) constructing the item.
\par \tab The cost of any special processes or materials is not included in the base cost to create the item, so if a wizard discovers that he must crush one hundred pearls to create his }{\i\fs24 dust of disappearance}{\fs24 , it\rquote 
s up to him to find the pearls. The time requirements do not include any special quests or processes the character must undertake in order to create the item.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Success or Failure: }{\fs24 All magical items that fall into this broad category share the same success roll. Assuming the character follows all the necessary steps, there is a base 60% chance of success, +1% per caster level, \endash 
1% per spell or special process required to create the item. (The caster\rquote s initial }{\i\fs24 enchant an item }{\fs24 or finishing }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 spells do not coun
t against his success chance.) While specialist wizards receive a 5% bonus to their success chance when creating an item using abilities from their school of specialization, artificers gain a special 10% bonus to their success chance due to their superior
 item\_crafting skills.
\par \tab At the DM\rquote s option, characters who display exceptional ingenuity or go to extreme lengths to create an item from the very best, most appropriate materials and processes available may receive an additional bonus of +5% to +15% on thei
r success chance.
\par \tab If the character rolls a 96 or higher on his success check, the item is cursed in some way. For example, a character attempting to produce a }{\i\fs24 cloak of displacement }{\fs24 might create a }{\i\fs24 cloak of poisonousness }{\fs24 
instead. If for some reason the character was trying to create a cursed item, a roll of 96 or higher is a simple failure\emdash he doesn\rquote t create a beneficial device instead!
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Thedaric is a 14th\_level fire mage who decides to create a }{\fs24 wand of fire }{\i\fs24 for those times when he\rquote s low on memorized spells.
 He easily meets the level requirement and spends a little money refurbishing a laboratory (in this case, a forge) that he found in the ruins of an archmage\rquote s tower. The forge comes with a library sufficient for researching the }{\fs24 
wand of fire }{\i\fs24 (it\rquote s the first item Thedaric has tried to make). Thedaric uses a contact other plane spell to minimize his research effort, so he only requires two weeks and 400 gp to learn how to create the wand.
\par \tab As described in the }{\fs24 DMG}{\i\fs24 , the }{\fs24 wand of fire }{\i\fs24 is a multiple\_function item (it has four separate uses), and a limited\_
use item, since it uses charges. The DM decides that the wand requires one exotic material, and four exotic processes, in addition to the necessary spells, cost, and time. Thedaric discovers that the wand must be f
orged by a master smith of the azer (a race of fire\_
dwarves from the Elemental Plane of Fire), from brass smelted in the efreeti City of Brass, tempered by the fiery breath of an adult red dragon, graven while still soft with runes of power, using a fire s
apphire (a mythical gemstone the DM just made up on the spot), and finally polished with a mixture containing the ash of a thousand year\_
old tree destroyed by fire. With a heavy sigh, Thedaric sets out on months of quests, challenges, and adventures, arranging all of the materials and processes.
\par \tab Several months later, everything\rquote s ready. Thedaric journeys to the elemental plane of fire, obtains the efreeti brass, gets the azer smith to work it into a wand, engraves it with the fire sapphire, tricks a dragon
 into tempering it, and finally polishes and finishes the item in his own workshop with his special mixture of ash. The construction of the item required an amount of gold equal to one\_fifth the wand\rquote 
s XP value (900 gp in this case) and 4 weeks per 100 gp, for a total of 36 weeks of forging, tempering, and polishing!
\par \tab Thedaric is well\_satisfied with his work so far, but now he has to make the wand magical. First, he\rquote ll need to use enchant an item in order to prepare the wand to receive spells. After four da
ys, the enchant an item is finished, and Thedaric attempts a saving throw vs. spell to see if it succeeded. His elementalist bonuses to saving throws vs. fire apply, and Thedaric passes with a surprisingly close shave. He then casts }{\fs24 
burning hands, pyrotechnics, fireball}{\i\fs24 , and }{\fs24 wall of fire }{\i\fs24 
into the wand. Each spell requires 2d4 hours per spell level, so this ends up taking several days in and of itself. Since he must check the success of each enchantment and doesn\rquote t know if any one spell will take, Thedaric c
asts another battery of the same spells into the wand, just to make sure that he gets all the functions desired\emdash 
at worst, the wand will have a few extra charges on it, so this is a reasonable precaution against the possibility of failing in one of these steps. Since the }{\fs24 wand of fire }{\i\fs24 is a limited\_use item, it does not require a }{\fs24 permanency 
}{\i\fs24 spell to complete it; after his second round of spells, Thedaric declares that he is finished. (He\rquote s not worried about stocking up on charges right now; he just wants to co
mplete the initial enchantment, and recharge the wand to its maximum potential later.)
\par \tab The DM rolled saving throws vs. spells for each spell Thedaric placed into the wand, and as it turns out, the extra four spells were an unnecessary precaution; Thedaric
 succeeded the first time around. Now the DM checks to see if the overall process was a success or failure. The base chance of success for a wand is 60%, plus 14% for Thedaric\rquote s level, \endash 
12% for spells and special processes. The DM decides that Thedaric was
 particularly resourceful, and gives him a +10% bonus, and since Thedaric is a fire specialist, he gains an additional +5% bonus, for a total success chance of 77%. Thedaric succeeds and now has a }{\fs24 wand of fire}{\i\fs24 
; the DM decides that the spells he placed into the wand became its first 8 charges (each spell was cast into the wand twice.)
\par \tab Now, Thedaric will probably seek to recharge the wand. Recharging items requires another enchant an item spell, but this one is automatically successful. He can then begin to 
place spells into the wand to increase the number of charges, up to its maximum of 50. So, after close to a year of adventuring and construction, Thedaric finishes his }{\fs24 wand of fire}{\i\fs24 
! Considering the immense time and effort this took a 14th\_level character, you can see why magical items should be rare and unusual things!
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Qualities}{\fs24 
\par \tab When a wizard or priest creates a magical item, he spends a lot of time and effort seeking a way to impart to his creation the particular qualities and properties he desires. While an exhaustive listing of each standard item\rquote 
s usual components would be beyond the scope of this book (and fairly boring, as well!), we\rquote ll take a quick look at some good ideas for components, spells, and processes designed to imbue an item with the powers the character desires.
\par \tab Qualities are divided into twelve loose categories: control or domination, charm or influence, perception, bodily alteration, bodily augmentation, movement, resistance and defense, attack or offense, summoning, object alteration, 
healing and restoration, and magical manipulation. Most items fall into at least one, and sometimes two, of these categories.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Control or Charm: }{\fs24 Magical devices of this sort exert a compulsion of some kind, forcing compliance from the subject. Unlike items
 that rely on influence or the power of emotion, control devices allow the caster to dictate commands to the subject, which will then be followed to the letter. Good examples of items that fall into this category include }{\i\fs24 
potions of giant, dragon, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 undead control}{\fs24 , or a }{\i\fs24 ring of mammal control }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 elemental control}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Materials for these items often include specimens or samples from the creature in question\emdash blood, hair, sweat, or more intangible qualities. Rare or exotic requirements might force the PC to seek
 out a unique individual among the subject race, such as a frost giant jarl, or a vampire mage.
\par \tab Rings, rods, and staves of this class might require decorating or engraving with a rune signifying the true, secret name of the subjects to be affected. A substance that is linked to the subject in some way could be included; for example, a }{
\i\fs24 potion of plant control }{\fs24 might require the sap of a treant since treants have the ability to animate other plants, or the potion might have to be prepared in a vessel carved from a treant\rquote 
s heartwood. Similarly, a powdered gem taken from a king\rquote s crown might be required for a }{\i\fs24 potion of human control}{\fs24 .
\par \tab In many cases, some form of }{\i\fs24 charm, geas}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 quest }{\fs24 spell will be required to enchant the item.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Influence or Emotion: }{\fs24 Magical dev
ices with these properties enable the wielder to exert unusual influence over the subject or impart an emotional state of some kind without gaining the ability to direct and control his movements. It is a subtler type of enchantment than outright control 
or domination, with more persistent effects that often highlight a player\rquote s role\_
playing ability. The wielder of the item is not able to actually order the subjects about but instead presents the subject with strong preferences or impulses that the subject 
is free to pursue as he sees fit. The least subtle of these items simply delivers an overwhelming emotion, such as fear or panic, to send the victims into instant flight. A }{\i\fs24 ring of human influence, wand of fear}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 
philter of love }{\fs24 all fall into this category.
\par \tab Unlike the control and charm devices, many devices in this category enhance the caster\rquote s Charisma or eloquence, without regard to the subject\rquote 
s race. Materials associated with the emotion required are often incorporated into magical items of this type; for example, a }{\i\fs24 wand of fear }{\fs24 
might require a bone from a lich or the terror of a coward. Items that confer persuasiveness to a character might require something from a creature with natural }{\i\fs24 charm }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 beguiling}{\fs24  powers, such as a snake\rquote 
s tongue or wood from the tree of a dryad.
\par \tab Processes could include such things as etching the item with the tears of a liar, engraving it with the secret name of a terrifying fiend, or tempering it on the altar of a deity of love or trickery. Rods, rings, and wands of this typ
e are often chased with metals related to the emotions in question\emdash  silver or gold for noble emotions, lead or iron for base ones.
\par \tab Spells that may prove useful in enchanting these items include such things as }{\i\fs24 animal friendship, emotion, enthrall, fear, suggestion}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 mass suggestion}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Perception: }{\fs24 Magical items of this type extend the wearer or user\rquote 
s perceptions in some way, enabling him to detect things he could not detect before, or extending the range of his senses beyond his immediate surroundings. Devices that expand the senses include such things as a }{\i\fs24 ring of x\_
ray vision, a medallion of ESP}{\fs24 , or a }{\i\fs24 gem of seeing}{\fs24 ; items that extend the senses include }{\i\fs24 potions of clairaudience }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance, crystal balls}{\fs24 , and similar items.
\par \tab Materials often include sam
ples or specimens from creatures who naturally possess the sense in question, such as the wit of a thief, the cunning of a fox, or the pick of a dwarf master miner. In addition, gems, glass, or stones of special clarity or color are often incorporated int
o devices of this nature.
\par \tab The processes required may involve more specimens of appropriate origin, such as polishing a magical lens with a paste made from the eye of a giant eagle or steeping a robe of eyes in the ichor of an argus. Other processes could i
nclude such things as grinding lenses or orbs, sanding items with special mixtures or compounds, magnetizing metal wands, or painting or inscribing an item in a certain design.
\par \tab All kinds of divination spells\emdash }{\i\fs24 clairaudience, clairvoyance, ESP, detect lie}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 true seeing}{\fs24 , for example\emdash may be part of the item creation process.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Movement: }{\fs24 A great number of magical items impart some supernatural means of travel. Some merely augment the wearer\rquote s natural abilities, while others open up entire new avenues of movemen
t for the character. There are a number of movement\_enhancing items, including }{\i\fs24 potions of flying and levitation, boots of speed, boots of striding and springing, carpet of flying, cloak of the bat, wings of flying}{\fs24 , and many others.

\par \tab 
Once again, specimens from creatures possessing the desired abilities are often important materials. Feathers from rare or unusual birds are frequently used for flying magic, while creatures such as grells or beholders provide levitation properties. Other
 materials could be more fantastic, such as the essence of the north wind.
\par \tab Depending on the nature of the item, the process usually serves to seal the magical power into the item. Boots might be stained with a special mixture or soled by a particular craft
sman or a special tool. Cloaks might be cured or waterproofed in some unusual way.
\par \tab Useful spells for items conferring movement powers include enchantments such as }{\i\fs24 jump, haste, fly, levitate, teleport, polymorph self, wind walk, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 plane shift}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Bodily Alteration: }{\fs24 This common category for magical items imparts some ability or power not normally possessed by the wearer. These abilities are not necessarily of
fensive or defensive, but they can provide the character with unusual resistances or camouflage in certain situations. Magical items that fall into this category include }{\i\fs24 potions of diminution, growth, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 gaseous form}{\fs24 
; items that confer }{\i\fs24 invisibility, blending, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 disguise }{\fs24 abilities; and items that provide the wearer with }{\i\fs24 water breathing, adaptation, }{\fs24 
or the ability to change his own shape. Naturally, this category often overlaps with several others since the alteration of one\rquote s form can augment the wearer\rquote s powers of movement, attack, or defense.
\par \tab In addition to materials harvested from creatures with the desired abilities, inert objects with the desired properties can be used as materials for these items. For example, a diamond or crystal of perfect clarity 
might be useful for invisibility, while the smallest grain of sand on a beach (now there\rquote s a challenge!) might be required for diminution. Steam from a certain volcano, or wood from a vampire\rquote s coffin, could impart gaseous form.
\par \tab Since many of the items in
 this category are potions, any process that is reasonable for creating a potion could be used. Other items might be steeped in special solutions designed to imbue them with the desired powers, or polished or painted with the materials required.
\par \tab Obviously, most of these items have spells that are immediately applicable to the enchantment. }{\i\fs24 Enlarge }{\fs24 or its reverse are good for diminution and growth; }{\i\fs24 invisibility}{\fs24 , }{\i\fs24 water breathing, polymorph self}{
\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 change self }{\fs24 may all be useful for items of this type.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Bodily Augmentation: }{\fs24 
Items of this type increase abilities or skills that the wielder already possesses by making him stronger and more dexterous, increasing his effective level, or augmenting his skills in a specific way. Examples include a }{\i\fs24 potion of giant str
ength }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 heroism}{\fs24 , a }{\i\fs24 girdle of giant strength}{\fs24 , }{\i\fs24 bracers of archery}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 gauntlets of dexterity}{\fs24 
. The chief difference between this category and the previous one is that augmentation changes existing abilities, while alteration provides abilities the character would not otherwise have.
\par \tab There are three major classes of item that can augment the user\rquote s natural abilities: potions, girdles and gauntlets, and books. Potions often feature the hair, blood, or sweat of a creature possessing the desired qualities\emdash a gian
t of the appropriate type for a }{\i\fs24 potion of giant strength}{\fs24  or a great hero for a }{\i\fs24 potion of heroism}{\fs24 
. Materials for persistent items might include such things as an arrow carved by a master elf fletcher, leather from the belt of a giant chieftain, or steel worked by the strongest ogre in the land.
\par \tab The processes required for potions have been described at length already. Belts, gauntlets, and other such things require curing, cutting and shaping, etching or inscribing, piercing, applying metal studs or fastener
s, and finishing with various rubs or mixtures.
\par \tab Spells that the character may find useful include enchantments such as }{\i\fs24 strength, bless, prayer}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 spider climb}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Resistance or Defense:}{\fs24  This large category includes all kinds of devices that provide the use
r with a resistance, defense, or immunity to some attack form. These can be divided into two subcategories: physical defenses, which protect the user from direct attack, and magical defenses, which negate specific forms of damage. A few items in this clas
s provide some benefits against both physical and magical attack. Examples of items with resistance or defensive powers include all kinds of magical armor, }{\i\fs24 potions of fire resistance }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 invulnerability, }{\fs24 
the various sorts of protection scrolls, }{\i\fs24 rings of mind shielding, sustenance}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 protection, cloaks of protection }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 displacement}{\fs24 , and many others.
\par \tab Naturally, favored materials include those that are resistant to the type of damage defended against by the item. These can be minerals or substances that possess the qualities desired\emdash 
diamonds for hardness, special clay or crystal for acid resistance, various metals and alloys for strength and resilience\emdash or samples from a creature known for a certain defense, such as the hide of a displacer beast or blink dog, th
e scale of a dragon, or the shell of a giant tortoise. Finally, substances inimical to the creature could be used to make a ward; garlic, holy symbols, or holy water could be incorporated into an }{\i\fs24 amulet versus undead}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Intangible materials such as a knight\rquote s courage, a moonbeam, or the morning mists on a sylvan lake may be required instead of physical substances. A }{\i\fs24 scarab versus golems }{\fs24 
might require the animating spark of a flesh golem, or the pity of an iron golem.
\par \tab Items of this class take many shapes and forms, but potions, armor, clothing, and jewelry are the most common varieties. The processes involved depend on the exact form of the item. However, processes designed to lend strength\emdash 
 tempering, shellacking or enamelling, or bonding\emdash are frequently used to finish these items. Any number of spells provide defenses or resistances of some kind; these may be useful in the creation process.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Healing and Restoration: }{\fs24 Resistances generally prevent injury from taking place, but magic of this category concentrate
s on the swift repair of damage or adverse conditions. Some types of item instead offer enhanced health or longevity; in general, if an item affects the metabolism of the wearer for the purpose of preserving his health, it falls into this category instead
 of bodily augmentation. Items in this category include }{\i\fs24 potions of healing }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 longevity, elixirs of health and vitality, ring of regeneration, staff of curing, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 periapts of health }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 
wound closure}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Many of these devices or brews require herbs, which ar
e special medicines and preparations famed for their healing potency. These herbs may require special harvesting or treatment before they can be incorporated into an item. In addition, animal samples from creatures who enjoy the properties in question can
 be useful; for example, a }{\i\fs24 ring of regeneration }{\fs24 may require the heart of a troll, while very long\_lived creatures (elves, treants, or dragons) may be useful for magic that prolongs life.
\par \tab Adventurers are most familiar with potions of this type, and these require the same steps or processes that other potions do\emdash 
distilling, brewing, aging, purification, and so on. Spells of healing and restoration are often required for creating items of this class, which means that most of these devices are created by priest characters.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Attack or Offense: }{\fs24 
As the largest single category of magical items, these devices with offensive powers range from simple enchanted weapons to mighty staves with a dazzling array of dreadful powers. Most rods, staves, and wands fall into this
 category along with almost all weapons and a fair number of rings, potions, and miscellaneous magical items. Just like defensive items, attack devices provide the wielder with either combat bonuses or magical effects, and a few (such as a }{\i\fs24 
staff of power}{\fs24 ) provide both.
\par \tab Weapons and other items designed to strike blows at an enemy usually rely on materials designed to grant extraordinary strength, sharpness, flexibility, or lightness and ease of use. Special minerals for the weapon\rquote s alloy are quite common. 
In addition, weapons with special qualities (}{\i\fs24 quickness, wounding, hurling}{\fs24 , and so on) may include samples from creatures that naturally possess these powers; a }{\i\fs24 sword of life stealing }{\fs24 
might require the essence of a wraith, while a }{\i\fs24 mace of disruption }{\fs24 could incorporate the holy symbol of a patriarch dead 1,000 years.
\par \tab Devices that project magical attacks at the wielder\rquote s enemy often require materials that reflect their nature. A }{\i\fs24 wand of frost }{\fs24 could be made from an icicle, the bones of a frost giant shaman, or the fang of a white dragon. A 
}{\i\fs24 staff of thunder and lightning }{\fs24 might require wood taken from a lightning\_struck treant; a }{\i\fs24 ring of shocking grasp }{\fs24 
that is etched with a solution made from the blood of electric eels is also appropriate. Obviously, there is a wonderful variety of ideas to choose from!
\par \tab The process involved reflects the item in some way. Again, items meant to be employed as weapons will often feature some kind of tempering or strengthening, while other items could be finished in any number of ways
. Items of this type that require charges may need certain spells to be cast into them over and over again during the creation process.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Magical Manipulation: }{\fs24 Magical items that affect other magical items, provide magical powers to their owners, or somehow
 augment or enhance the spell capability of their owners belong to this group. These items are among the most potent in the AD&D game system. This category includes }{\i\fs24 rings of spell storing, spell turning, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 wizardry}{\fs24 ; }
{\i\fs24 rods of absorption and cancellation; wand of negation; pearl of power; incense of meditation; book of infinite spells}{\fs24 ; and the }{\i\fs24 beaker of plentiful potions}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Generally, items of this sort require either highly magical or highly antimagical materials since they are designed to manipulate the ver
y stuff of magic itself. Magical materials include special alloys of meteoric or extraplanar minerals, as well as things such as a unicorn\rquote s horn, a kirin\rquote 
s hooves, the bones of an archmage, or the holy symbol of a saint. Antimagical materials could consist of specimens from creatures with high magic resistance, iron taken from a nonmagical prime material world, or wood from a tree rooted in a magic\_
dead area in worlds where such places exist.
\par \tab Processes suitable for items of this type may involve polishing
 or etching with a solution of magical or antimagical substances, tempering or engraving it in a place of great magical potential (the extraplanar domain of a god of magic, for example), or bathing it in the raw stuff of magic, such as a wild mage\rquote 
s }{\i\fs24 wildfire }{\fs24 spell.
\par \tab Spells suitable for empowering magic\_manipulating items include dweomers such as }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell, dispel magic, Mordenkainen\rquote s disjunction, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 spell turning}{\fs24 . Priests may rely on }{\i\fs24 
imbue with spell ability }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 holy word}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Matter Manipulation: }{\fs24 Items of this sort are designed to have their greatest effects on inanimate objects or substances by transforming, destroying, or otherwise altering something without making a direct attack. Matter\_
manipulating devices include }{\i\fs24 potion of sweetwater, oil of timelessness, wand of flame extinguishing, decanter of endless water, maul of the titans, }{\fs24 or the }{\i\fs24 horn of collapsing}{\fs24 
. While many of these devices have obvious applications as weapons in certain situations, in most cases this is an incidental benefit or hazard of their normal function.
\par \tab Materials for these items often consist of substances that have the effect desired or animal samples from creatures that can perform the intended action. For example, a }{\i\fs24 wand of flame extinguishing }{\fs24 
could incorporate ice or water from the heart of the Elemental Plane of Water, while a }{\i\fs24 spade of colossal excavation }{\fs24 might require the ground\_up claws of a giant badger to be mixed into the alloy for the shovel\rquote 
s blade. In a couple of cases, the item contains some kind of link to one of the elemental planes and produces an endless supply of one substance or another.
\par \tab The process varies with the type of item; tools may require balancing, sharpening, or tempering of some kind. Spells that may be useful include }{\i\fs24 dig, move earth, temporal stasis, purify food and drink, }{\fs24 
and other spells designed to affect objects.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Summoning: }{\fs24 Items that summon monsters, servants, or champions to aid the wielder fall into the class of summoning devices. In addition to the devices which obviously bring creatures from distant locales, }{\i\fs24 
figurines of wondrous power }{\fs24 and other objects that transform into living servitors can be considered summoning devices since the overall effect (i.e., the user gains a useful ally of some kind) is much the same. Other summoning items include a }{
\i\fs24 ring of djinni summoning, staff of swarming insects, brazier commanding fire elementals, pipes of the sewers}{\fs24 , and the }{\i\fs24 horn of Valhalla}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Summoning devices almost always include materials that are pleasing to the creatures to be commanded, or at least signify them in some way. For example, a }{\i\fs24 horn of Valhalla }{\fs24 
might require the courage of a berserker, iron from the riven shield of a mighty hero, or gold won from a dragon\rquote s hoard. Devices built to summon extraplanar monsters often feature material collected on the subject creature\rquote s home plane.

\par \tab The finishing processes of a summoning device usually reinforce the bond with the particular creature by bathing or steeping the item in the creature\rquote s blood or by somehow imbuing 
it with a substance desired by the monster. Several spells may prove useful in creating these items, including }{\i\fs24 gate, exaction, entrapment, conjure elemental}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 binding}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Items That No Player Character 
\par Should Create
\par }{\fs24 \tab The rules for creating magical items a
llow the DM a great amount of latitude in determining just how difficult the creation of any particular item is going to be. After all, the search for a few exotic materials and processes can keep an ambitious PC busy for years! And the rules have been sc
aled to make the most powerful and deadly items prohibitively expensive and time\_consuming. But, despite these safeguards, there are still a few items that are just too powerful for a player character to create.
\par \tab The first item is actually an entire category\emdash 
magical books. Books, tomes, librams, manuals, and other such items provide the character with the ability to build himself an instant level gain or quickly enhance his ability scores. In most campaigns, it\rquote 
s safe to assume that magical books are demi\_artifacts endowed with a special purpose and a near\_
sentient talent for skipping out on their present owners. For whatever reasons, powers beyond mortal ken had a hand in the creation of these devices, and the only characters who can duplicate the feat are those who stand on the verge of divine ascension.

\par \tab The next two items are simply so inexplicably weird that the reasons why a PC would wish to create one or the other is almost impossible to fathom. These are the }{\i\fs24 deck of many things }{\fs24 and the }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation}{\fs24 
. Both have potentially disastrous consequences for a campaign and should be extremely rare in any event. In particular, players seem to have a vindictive streak with regard to }{\i\fs24 spheres of annihilation}{\fs24 
, using them to do things like drain seas and devour planets. The DM is well advised to prevent PCs from making a few dozen of these to liven up his world. The }{\i\fs24 deck of many things}{\fs24 
, on the other hand, offers instant level gains and other such game\_busting benefits. If a group of PCs finds one after an arduous adventure and chooses to experiment with it, they\rquote ve earned the privilege. But preparing a new }{\i\fs24 deck }{
\fs24 on demand is a privilege too dangerous for most players.
\par \tab The last category is the least spectacular: special racial magic, such as }{\i\fs24 cloaks }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 boots of elvenkind}{\fs24 . Unless the character is a member of the race in question, he shouldn\rquote 
t be able to create these items. However, if the character is an elf (for example), and he\rquote s a priest that has risen high enough in level to contemplate the manufacture of these devices, there\rquote s no reason the DM couldn\rquote 
t allow him to do so as long as he follows the normal rules for item creation.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Recharging Magical Items}{\fs24 
\par \tab Items that possess charges, such as most wands or rods, can be recharged. Some item descriptions name the particular class and level of character that can recharge the item; for example, a }{\i\fs24 rod of security }{\fs24 
can only be recharged by the combined efforts of a wizard and priest of 18th level or higher. If the item description does not specify the level required to recharge it, then it can be recharged by any character who can create an item (i.e., an 11th\_
level priest, or a wizard with the ability to cast }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 ), as long as the character can also cast the highest\_level spell simulated or cast by the item. For example, the highest\_level spell incorporated in a }{\i\fs24 
wand of fire}{\fs24  is }{\i\fs24 wall of fire}{\fs24 , a 4th\_level wizard spell, which can be cast by a wizard of 7th level or higher; therefore, if the wizard knows }{\i\fs24 wall of fire}{\fs24 , it\rquote s the requirement to }{\i\fs24 
enchant an item }{\fs24 that is the pressing issue in this case.
\par \tab If the character can use the appropriate spells, an item can be recharged by simply casting }{\i\fs24 enchant an item }{\fs24 and then making a saving throw vs. spell to see if the spell takes. (Priests spend a week praying at their consecr
ated altar to recharge their items.) If the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 
 succeeds, the character may then cast the appropriate spells into the item, taking 1d4 hours per spell. If the preparatory spell fails, then the caster must make another saving throw vs. spell with a \endash 
1 penalty this time; failing this save results in the item\rquote s ruin. It will never be usable again.
\par \tab Assuming the character doesn\rquote t spoil the item by attempting to recharge it, he may then begin to place spells of the appropriate type into the item. As 
long as he begins the next spell within 24 hours of finishing the last one, he can pour charges into the item without stopping to use }{\i\fs24 enchant an item }{\fs24 
again. Success is automatic, and the character can place as many (or as few) additional charges into the item as he likes. However, he may never overcharge the item by placing more charges into it than it could have at its maximum. For example, a }{
\i\fs24 ring of the ram }{\fs24 can hold as many as 10 charges at one time, so it can\rquote t be recharged past that limit.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs36\cf6 Chapter 8:
\par Spells in Combat
\par 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 While many spel
ls provide a character with information, influence, or unusual powers of movement, most wizards and priests seem to be interested in spells that bring doom and destruction upon their enemies. Naturally, there are a great number of these enchantments, rang
ing from spells that annoy or hinder the subjects to awesome strikes that can slay dozens of monsters at once.
\par \tab In this chapter, we\rquote ll examine the use of spells on the battlefield. First of all, we\rquote ll take a look at characteristics or properties of combative spells\emdash 
for example, how far away can a fireball be seen? Next, spells with special or unusual effects associated with them will be dealt with\emdash just what does a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 
do to the air quality of a small room? For those of our readers who are familiar with the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 book, we\rquote 
ll examine the issue of spells that cause knockdowns and the possibility of critical strikes or specific injuries caused by spell damage.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Spell Characteristics  }{\fs24 If you\rquote ve played the AD&D game for any amount o
f time, you are already quite familiar with a number of spell characteristics such as range, duration, area of effect, and other such attributes. Some characteristics are fixed values, while others may vary with the caster\rquote s level. }{\i\fs24 Player
\rquote s Option: Spells & Magic }{\fs24 introduces the new characteristics of }{\b\fs24 casting subtlety }{\fs24 and }{\b\fs24 sensory signature}{\fs24 , which describe the obviousness of a spell\rquote s casting or effects.
\par \tab In addition to perception characteristics, we\rquote ll also take a look at spells that may create knockdowns (a concept from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 
book), as well as spells that require attack rolls on the part of the caster.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Casting Subtlety}{\fs24 
\par \tab In many situations, a wizard or priest couldn\rquote t care less who sees him casting a spell. When an archmage is getting ready to electrocute a band of ogres with his }{\i\fs24 chain lightning }{\fs24 spell, he usually doesn\rquote 
t take any steps to conceal what he is doing and casts the spell in the most expedient fashion possible. On the other hand, some situations require great delicacy on the part of the character. When a wizard wants to }{\i\fs24 charm }{\fs24 a guard\_
captain in front of the captain\rquote s detachment of soldiers, he\rquote ll have to be very careful not to tip his hand.
\par \tab Naturally, some spells are more subtle than others. There are a number of factors to take into consideration\emdash the number of components required, the size of the material com
ponents, the length of the casting time, and whether or not the spell requires some unusual behavior or speech on the part of the caster.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Noticing Spells: }{\fs24 Who notices when spells are being cast? Generally, anyone who is directly observing the caster notic
es the strange gestures or air of concentration that characterize a spell. Depending on the lighting conditions and the amount of distractions nearby, an observer might be able to notice the spellcaster\rquote 
s actions from as far as 30 to 50 feet away. Casual observers\emdash people who are on the scene, but don\rquote t have any particular reason to be watching the spellcaster\emdash rarely notice the spell unless it\rquote s unusually obvious. If it\rquote 
s important to know whether or not a particular NPC or monster notices the spellcaster\rquote s actions, the DM can make an Intelligence check for that character (Intelligence acts as a measure of a character\rquote s perceptiveness).
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Subtlety Rating: }{\fs24 A spell\rquote s subtlety rating is the overall measure of how easily the caster\rquote s actions are detected by an obs
erver. In some cases, the spell is automatically detected; any character within 10 feet who }{\i\fs24 specifically }{\fs24 
watches the spellcaster (for example, someone engaged in melee with the caster, or a vigilant guard) will automatically notice that the character is at
tempting a spell. However, if an Intelligence check is used to determine whether or not a character notices a spell, the subtlety rating is used as a modifier to the character\rquote s roll.
\par \tab The spell\rquote s subtlety rating is figured as shown in Table 39: Spell Subtlety Modifiers.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Table 39:
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Spell Subtlety Modifiers
\par }{\fs24 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\b\fs24 Modifier\tab Condition
\par }{\fs24 +1\tab Casting time of 4 or higher
\par +2\tab Casting time of 7 or higher
