\par 
\par \tab Casting time is an import
ant factor in the subtlety of a spell, since the longer the character is involved in the spell, the more likely it is that someone will notice him. The number of components involved measures how complicated the spell is and how difficult it is to cast. La
st but not least, some spells clearly require some unusual actions or behaviors that may be hard to disguise. However, if the caster can mask his actions by performing them in a place or manner that wouldn\rquote 
t be suspicious (for example, dancing or clapping in a street carnival, or shouting on a crowded battlefield) this last modifier can be negated.
\par \tab The spell\rquote s subtlety rating is the total of its modifiers. It is applied to an observer\rquote s Intelligence when rolling an Intelligence check to see if he or she not
ices the physical act of spellcasting. Remember, in many cases an Intelligence check is completely unnecessary\emdash a wizard who casts a spell in a pitch\_black room won\rquote 
t be seen by anyone, while a priest who uses magic in a crowded town market is probably going to be noticed by someone nearby.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Vorgaad, a necromancer, is enjoying a stout ale in his favorite taproom when a pushy paladin challenges him over some trifling issue of raising decently buried townsfolk and using them for his own evil ends. Vorgaad sig
hs and orders his minion Azoth to accept the paladin\rquote s challenge. Accompanied by a large number of tavern\_goers, all concerned step outside into the torchlit yard of the inn. Vorgaad decides that the paladin will cut Azoth to pieces if he doesn
\rquote t do something and decides to use }{\fs24 stoneskin }{\i\fs24 on Azoth to even the odds. }{\fs24 Stoneskin }{\i\fs24 
has a casting time of 1 and requires verbal, somatic, and material components. As a result, its total subtlety rating is 4. In addition, the DM rules that the act of sprinkling granite a
nd diamond dust over Azoth is strange enough for an additional +1 bonus, giving anyone nearby a +5 bonus to their chance to detect Vorgaad\rquote 
s spell. However, the light in the courtyard, the din of the crowd, and the spectacle of Azoth taunting the paladin a
ll help Vorgaad remain unnoticed; taken together, these reduce the bonus to 0. Any bystander who succeeds in an Intelligence check sees Vorgaad cast his spell.
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Note that characters can sometimes deceive intelligent enemies by }{\i\fs24 pretending }{\fs24 to cast spells. The would\_be trickster should have some idea of what he\rquote s doing; otherwise he\rquote 
s just flailing his arms around and shouting nonsense (which might be a distraction in and of itself!) It also helps a lot if the target of the charade has seen that particular ch
aracter use magic before since the whole act is much more credible when it follows a }{\i\fs24 real }{\fs24 spell. Consider how fast a band of trolls might scatter if they\rquote d just been }{\i\fs24 fireballed }{\fs24 
and then observe the same wizard getting ready to cast another spell!
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Sensory Signature}{\fs24 
\par \tab Another characteristic of spells is their sensory signature, or just how noticeable the effects of the spell are. A }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt }{\fs24 
includes a blinding flash of light and a deafening clap of thunder; clearly, it is much more noticeable than a }{\i\fs24 shocking grasp }{\fs24 or an }{\i\fs24 unseen servant}{\fs24 . Many spell effects are completely undetectable; for example, a }{
\i\fs24 charm person, polymorph self}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 feeblemind }{\fs24 spell may wreak all kinds of havoc with the subject, but outside observers can\rquote t see, smell, or hear anything when 
the spell is cast that lets them know that magic was just employed. Sensory effects fall into (naturally) five major categories: sight, hearing, smell, touch, and taste.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sight:}{\fs24  Spells that create matter, project energy, or create visible changes in the subject have a visual sensory signature. Any creature close enough to see the effect will be aware of the fact that a spell has been cast.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hearing: }{\fs24 Most spells with visible signatures also have an audible signature as well, even if it\rquote s a weird hum, buzz, or }{\i\fs24 ZOTTT! }{\fs24 
as the magic affects its target. A few spells have particularly noticeable audible signatures, such as }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt, shout}{\fs24 
, and other such deadly enchantments. Very few spells have an audible signature without some form of visual signature as well.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Smell: }{\fs24 Enchantments such as }{\i\fs24 stinking cloud }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 cloudkill }{\fs24 
are the obvious offenders in this category, but other spells may leave olfactory signatures behind, particularly spells that start fires. The pungent fumes of a }{\i\fs24 pyrotechnics }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 incendiary cloud }{\fs24 
spell may easily be the first thing an observer notices, even before he sees the smoke.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Touch and Taste: }{\fs24 Usually, if a character\rquote s close enough to notice a spell\rquote s effect by these methods, he\rquote s already quite aware of the fact that magic has been used agai
nst him. However, in some cases, this might be a character\rquote s only way to detect a spell. For example, a character who has been magically }{\i\fs24 blinded }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 deafened }{\fs24 would still feel the warmth of a distant }{\i\fs24 
fireball }{\fs24 on his face or feel the tremors caused by a priest\rquote s }{\i\fs24 earthquake}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Strength of Sensory Signatures}{\fs24 
\par \tab All sorts of conditions may influence the obviousness of a spell; a }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt }{\fs24 may be reduced to a dim, distant flash in a deep fog, but the crack of the thunderbolt will still be quite audible! The num
ber of creatures affected by a spell can be used as a rough measure of the strength of a sensory signature, as shown in Table 40: Sensory Signatures.
\par \tab For example, a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 normally affects a 20\_foot radius, so it would be considered a huge signature detectable at a range of 500 feet under good visibility conditions. On the other hand, }{\i\fs24 polymorph other }{\fs24 
affects only one target, so it would be a medium signature spotted at a range of 50 feet. Obviously, the DM has a great deal of latitude here; spells break the rules of the game, and many defy classification.
\par \tab If the visibility is poor, the ranges listed above could be reduced by as much as 50 to 75 percent. However, simple darkness may not conceal spells with brilliant or fiery effects; if anything, a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt
}{\fs24  is even more visible at nighttime than it is in daylight!
\par \tab Spells can usually be heard or smelled at half the range they can be seen and felt at one\_tenth the visibility range. The roar of a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 normally carries for 250 feet or so
, even if the blast itself is out of sight around a corner or behind a hill. A deafened character who isn\rquote t looking at the blast can still feel the heat of the }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 
from as far as 50 feet away. However, sound and smells may not propagate in a perfect circle from the spell\rquote s targeting point\emdash if there\rquote s a strong wind blowing, the faint stench of a }{\i\fs24 stinking cloud }{\fs24 
may be noticeable a mile or more downwind, while stone walls and large areas of still water may reflect or bounce sounds to several times their normal carrying distance.
\par 
