\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Sensory Signatures by School
\par }{\fs24 \tab While each school of magic includes a variety of different spells, it\rquote s possible to make some general observations about each school. Both priest and wizard spells of the listed type share some common sensory signatures.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Abjurations: }{\fs24 Spells of this type fall into three loose categories\emdash those that create barriers, those that enhance the  recipient\rquote s resistance to attack, and those that directly atta
ck the offending creature or object. Barriers include spells such as }{\i\fs24 protection from evil, minor globe of invulnerability, antimagic shell}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 prismatic sphere}{\fs24 
; generally, these spells have a normal visible signature for their size, but don\rquote t produce odd smells or sounds. Spells that enhance resistance include }{\i\fs24 sanctuary, nondetection, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 protection from normal missiles}{\fs24 
. These enchantments have small signatures or no discernible signatures at all. Abjurations that attack the offending creature include }{\i\fs24 fire trap, repulsion, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 banishment; }{\fs24 
they may include sight and sound signatures as normal for their area of effect. The following are some specific examples:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Protection from evil: }{\fs24 moderate visual;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Minor globe of invulnerability:}{\fs24  small visual;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Banishment: }{\fs24 small visual, small audio.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Alterations: }{\fs24 This broad school includes spells designed to accomplish a multitude of effects. As a gross generalization, all alterations have the signatures derived from their areas of effect with a couple of notable exceptions
. Alteration spells that affect other spells (the so\_called \ldblquote metamagic\rdblquote  spells, like }{\i\fs24 far reaching }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 Rary\rquote s mnemonic enhancer}{\fs24 ) and spells that imbue the caster with a quality that doesn
\rquote t change his appearance (}{\i\fs24 comprehend languages, fly, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 water breathing}{\fs24 , for example) usually have no signature at all. Specific examples appear below:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24\cf1 Burning hands: }{\fs24 moderate visual and audio;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Haste: }{\fs24 no signature at all;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Animal growth: }{\fs24 moderate visual and audio.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Conjurations: }{\fs24 Most conjurations involve visual sensory signatures since people on the scene notice whatever it is that just appeared, but audio signatures are unusual. Instead, olfactory signatures\emdash 
strange smells and odors\emdash  often accompany spells of this school. Also, tactile sensations such as chills, goosebumps, or nausea may come into play when doors to other dimensions are opened in the vicinity. The following are specific examples:

\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Unseen servant: }{\fs24 small audio and tactile;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Monster summoning: }{\fs24 moderate visual;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Power word: }{\fs24 small audio and tactile.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Divinations: }{\fs24 A great number of divinations have no sensory signature at all since they impart information directly to the caster\rquote s awareness. However, some divination spells (such as }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24 
) cause the items or objects in question to glow, so that anyone nearby can see them.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Enchantments: }{\fs24 Spells of this type that directly manipulate the subject\rquote 
s mental or emotional state have no signature to speak of, but a few spells of this type that relay compulsions or orders, or affect objects instead of people (}{\i\fs24 command, bind, ray of enfeeblement, snake charm, trip, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 snare, }{
\fs24 to name a few examples) have normal sensory signatures as appropriate for their areas of effect.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Charm person: }{\fs24 no sensory signature;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Hold person: }{\fs24 moderate visual/tactile signature;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Ray of enfeeblement: }{\fs24 small visual/audio signature.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Illusions: }{\fs24 In one sense, illusions are nothing more than sensory signatures with no reality attached to them. The central spells of this school\emdash }{\i\fs24 phantasmal force }{\fs24 
and its various improvements and refinements\emdash are designed to create the largest \ldblquote signature\rdblquote 
 possible and may easily be observed from quite a distance away. A few illusion spells work directly on the mind of the subject and have a much smaller sensory effect; these include }{\i\fs24 misdirection, phantasmal killer, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 eyebite}{
\fs24 . Illusions that mask or conceal something else have no true sensory signature in and of themselves; if an }{\i\fs24 invisible }{\fs24 wizard was surrounded by a glow or gained a pungent odor, the spell would be worthless!
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Phantasmal force: }{\fs24 visual signature by area affected;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Wraithform: }{\fs24 small visual/tactile signature;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Eyebite: }{\fs24 no sensory signature.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Invocations: }{\fs24 Without question, the school of invocation/ evocation includes the most spectacular spells available to a wizard or priest. Like it or not, a wizard who throws }{\i\fs24 fireballs}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 
lightning bolts }{\fs24 is creating a fireworks show that people can see and hear for quite a distance away. Blinding flashes of light and deafening peals of thunder leave no doubt in the observer\rquote s mind that magic is at work here. Here are some cl
assic examples:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Magic missile:}{\fs24  moderate visual signature;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Fireball: }{\fs24 huge visual, moderate audio/tactile;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Cloudkill: }{\fs24 large visual, large olfactory.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Necromancies: }{\fs24 While spells of this school can be extremely potent, the flash and bang of a powerful invocation isn\rquote 
t found in most necromancy spells. Most necromancy spells affect only a single subject at a time. Tactile sensations such as chills, nausea, or disorientat
ion are somewhat more common in necromancy than thunderous detonations or weird haloes of light. The following are some good examples of necromancy spells:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Enervation: }{\fs24 small visual/audio/tactile;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Animate dead: }{\fs24 moderate visual/tactile;
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Cure serious wounds: }{\fs24 small visual/tactile.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Spells with Attack Rolls}{\fs24 
\par \tab A small number of spells require the caster to make an attack roll in order to deliver damage or other effects to the victim. Some of these can be very potent, but their power is limited by the requiremen
t to actually touch the subject with a successful attack roll. Generally, the caster can execute a touch attack (or roll an attack roll) as soon as he finishes casting the spell, or he can delay until the end of the current round and then make his attack.
 However, if the caster doesn\rquote t attempt to discharge the spell in the round in which it is cast, it is wasted unless the spell has a duration of more than one round. For example, }{\i\fs24 cause light wounds }{\fs24 
must be used in the same round in which it is cast, but since }{\i\fs24 chill touch }{\fs24 lasts a minimum of four rounds, the caster doesn\rquote t have to \ldblquote use it or lose it\rdblquote  in the round he casts the spell.
\par \tab A number of spells don\rquote t require the caster to actually injure the target or breach the victim\rquote s armor; even a glancing touch will be sufficient to discharge the spell\rquote s effects on the target. Spells of this type }{\i\fs24 
ignore the portion of the victim\rquote s Armor Class that is derived from wearing armor}{\fs24 . In other words, only Dexterity\_based and magical adjustments help a character\rquote s Armor Class against some magical attacks.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Oromonos the wizard takes a dislike to an obnoxious guard and decides to teach him a lesson with a }{\fs24 shocking grasp}{\i\fs24 . Since the spell only requires the wizard to make contact with the target, the DM rules that the guard
\rquote s plate mail +1 won\rquote t help him. Only the 1\_point Armor Class bonus for the armor\rquote s enchantment counts for the guard\rquote s AC, which makes him AC 9 to Oromonos\rquote s attack instead of his usual AC 2.
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Which spells qualify as spells that bypass armor? Basically, this 
is a judgment call for the DM. In order to gain this advantage, the spell description should clearly imply that the spell would take effect even if it struck the victim on the breastplate instead of requiring the caster\rquote 
s hand to actually touch exposed ski
n. Another way of looking at it is this: Does the spell emulate the action of a normal weapon, such as a sword, arrow, or dagger? If it does, the spell definitely requires a normal attack roll on the part of the caster, but if the spell affects the victim
\rquote s life force or achieves its effect from simple contact, the subject\rquote s armor is ignored. 
\par \tab Spells that directly affect the subject\rquote s life force include most of the necromantic touch spells (}{\i\fs24 chill touch, vampiric touch, energy drain, }{\fs24 etc.), the various }{\i\fs24 cause wound }{\fs24 
spells and other reversals of healing spells such as }{\i\fs24 poison, slay living}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 destruction}{\fs24 . The victim\rquote s armor does not help to protect him from these attacks. Spells that somehow change or alter the victim
\rquote s status, such as }{\i\fs24 imprisonment, plane shift}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 dispel evil }{\fs24 also ignore armor.
\par \tab A second category of spells that can affect the victim through his armor includes spells that produce energy or forces against which armor is useless. For example, }{\i\fs24 shocking grasp, chromatic orb, watery double, produce flame, fire seeds, }{
\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 crystalbrittle }{\fs24 can affect an armored or unarmored person equally well.
\par \tab Some spells that use attack rolls but ignore the victim\rquote s armor appear in Table 41: Armor\_Breaching Spells.
\par \tab Spells that create physical attacks or emulate the effects of weapons include such enchantments as }{\i\fs24 ice knife, hovering skull, Mordenkainen\rquote s sword, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 spiritual hammer}{\fs24 
. Since these spells manifest as tangible weapons and are wielded in the same way, the subject is allowed the full benefit of his or her armor. See Table 42: Armor\_Observing Spells.
