\par \tab }{\b\i\fs24\cf6 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics: }{\fs24 In the revised initiative system introduced in the }{\i\fs24 Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 
book, spells that require attack rolls are cast as normal (i.e., on the fast, average, or slow phase, as appropriate for
 the casting time). After the spell is invoked, the caster may then make his attack for the round in any subsequent phase. For example, a wizard who casts }{\i\fs24 chill touch}{\fs24 
, a fast spell, may then make a touch attack in the average, slow, or very slow phases. Note that the character still receives only one attack per round.
\par \tab Even though the wizard will often be \lquote unarmed\rquote  when he uses a touch attack spell, he does }{\i\fs24 not }{\fs24 provoke an attack of opportunity since he\rquote s not attempting to initiate unarmed combat. All he nee
ds to do is touch the subject, not land a full\_force punch or wrestle with the fellow. However, a victim who is guarding will still be able to make his own attack before the wizard can deliver his touch\_based attack.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Knockdowns}{\fs24 
\par \tab The concept of knockdowns was introduced in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 
rulebook. Knockdowns are hits that send the victim flying or put him on the ground, knocking him prone. The ability to cause a knockdown varies from weapon to weapon, and the larger the target, the mo
re resistant he is to knockdown results. Very few attacks have the power to put a frost giant flat on his back!
\par \tab While a club\_wielding giant or a powerful barbarian obviously has the potential to take an opponent off his feet, many spells also have the abi
lity to drive a victim to the ground with tremendous force, shock, or concussion. In some cases, spell energy may cause knockdowns through spasms, intense pain, or physiological shock as well as sheer impact energy.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Knockdown Die: }{\fs24 
The likelihood of a weapon (or a spell) to cause a knockdown is governed by the size of the knockdown die. Any time a character suffers damage from a spell, the caster may roll a knockdown die to see if there is a chance for a knockdown. If the number rol
led on the knockdown die is higher than the victim\rquote s knockdown number, he must roll a saving throw vs. death magic or fall down.
\par \tab Usually, being knocked down forces the character to spend his next action or attack standing again. Standing is treated as moving half the character\rquote s normal movement rating, so a knocked\_
down fighter could choose to stand in the following round and still receive his normal allotment of melee attacks, while a knocked\_down archer who stood up would have to fire at 1/2 his normal rate of fire. (See }{\b\fs24 What You Can Do in One Round}{
\fs24 , page 122 of the }{\i\fs24 PHB}{\fs24 .)
\par \tab While a character is on the ground, he is especially vulnerable to attack. Anyone attacking the knocked\_down character gains a +4 bonus to hit, and the prone character may not apply his Dexterity adjustment (if any) to his Armor Class. Obviously, it
\rquote s worth the time to stand up again!
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Knockdown Number: }{\fs24 A character\rquote s resistance to knockdowns is measured by his knockdown number. Size is the most important characteristic here, although a ch
aracter with a good saving throw will avoid many knockdowns regardless of his size. See Table 43: Knockdown Numbers by Size.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Oromonos isn\rquote t done with that guard yet. Since the }{\fs24 shocking grasp }{\i\fs24 didn\rquote t deter the fellow, Oromonos throws a }{\fs24 lightning bolt }{\i\fs24 at him at point\_blank range. The }{\fs24 lightning bolt }{
\i\fs24 has a knockdown die of d12 (we\rquote ll discuss knockdown die sizes for particular spells in a moment), and Oromonos rolls an 8 with the d12 while he\rquote s rolling his damage for the spell. Since the guard is a human (size 
M) and therefore knocked down on a 7 or better, he must now make a saving throw vs. death magic or hit the turf. Although the fellow does save against the lightning bolt itself, he blows his knockdown save and winds up on the ground, wondering what hit hi
m.
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Spells and Knockdown Dice
\par }{\fs24 \tab Clearly, not every spell has the potential for a knockdown. Enchantments such as }{\i\fs24 charm person }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 slow }{\fs24 may create serious difficulties for the victim, but they\rquote 
re not going to throw him to the ground in the process. Other spells cause \lquote knockdowns\rquote  just by their nature; }{\i\fs24 sleep, grease}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 ice storm }{\fs24 
can put creatures on the ground by virtue of their normal effects. Table 44: Spells with Knockdown Effects list spells that actually use the knockdown rules.
\par \tab For spells from other sources, use your best judgment in assigning a knockdown die. First of all, the spell must deliver energy to the target or cause a physiological reaction that might cause the subject to lose control of his limbs\emdash 
for example, the agony associated with }{\i\fs24 Abi\_Dalzim\rquote s horrid wilting }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 throbbing bones }{\fs24 
has a chance to distract even the stoutest warrior, despite the fact that neither spell actually strikes the victim with a tangible blow. You may observe that }{\i\fs24 lightning bolts }{\fs24 are particularly good for knockdowns, beca
use they strike the target with an immense amount of energy and electrocute the victim at the same time!
\par \tab Spells that stun, daze, sleep, confuse, charm, hold, trip, fumble, or tangle the opponent don\rquote t need a knockdown die; if the spell succeeds, the victi
m is hindered by the action of the spell itself, and a knockdown would be redundant. Last but not least, spells that simulate weapons (}{\i\fs24 Mordenkainen\rquote s sword}{\fs24 , for example) can use a knockdown die based on the weapon itself; see }{
\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 for a complete listing of weapons and knockdown ratings.
\par 
\par 
