\par }{\fs28\cf12 Collateral Spell Effects  }{\fs24 Everyone knows that a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24  does 1d6 damage per level of the caster, but have you ever stopped to consider what else a ball of flame 40 feet across might do? First of all, a }{\i\fs24 
fireball }{\fs24 will start a lot of fires, especially in medieval settings with thatch\_roofed houses, garments redolent with animal oils and tanning chemicals, and lots of wooden construction. Secondly, the residual fires left by a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{
\fs24 will produce smoke\emdash probably a lot of it, as many fires smoke very heavily just when they\rquote re getting started. Next, a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 
may foul the air of a confined space by consuming a great amount of the available oxygen. But, for most players and DMs, the same fireball did 17 points of damage, and that was it!
\par \tab In this section, we\rquote ll take a quick look at unusual effects that may accompany the use of many powerful spells. In military terms, these incidental effects are referred to as collateral damage\emdash a PC wizard probably isn\rquote 
t trying to start a three\_alarm fire when he torches a city guard with his }{\i\fs24 burning hands }{\fs24 spell, but it might happen anyway. Depending on the DM\rquote 
s mischievousness, all kinds of mayhem may break loose when wizards start throwing heavy\_damage spells around.
\par \tab The most important characteristic of a spell for purposes of collateral effects is its general type and purpose. There isn\rquote t a whole lot of fallout from a }{\i\fs24 charm person }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 
spell, but many other spells create environmental effects that may be long\_lasting or even permanent.
\par }{\fs28\cf6 
\par Fire}{\fs24 
\par \tab A great number of spells employ fire as a weapon. Fires create smoke, foul contained atmospheres, and may spread to nearby flammables.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Visibility: }{\fs24 Flames don\rquote t produce smoke\emdash burning objects or creatures do. A }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24  thrown in an unfurnished room of bare stone against a creature that won\rquote 
t burn well (say, an ice elemental) will leave very little in the way of heavy smoke. On the other hand, a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 thrown in a sod house or a wooden building cluttered 
with greasy old furs and shoddy furniture will create a number of smoky residual fires and quickly cloud up the surroundings. Generally, a fire spell creates a cloud of smoke in its area of effect that persists for 2d4 rounds. This obscures vision, forcin
g all characters and monsters to suffer a \endash 2 penalty on missile attacks through the smoke. The stink of burning objects may also hinder creatures that rely on scent to detect their enemies.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Environmental Effects:}{\fs24  Fire spells naturally cause fires. See }{\b\fs24 Item Saving Throws }{\fs24 on page 58 of the }{\i\fs24 DMG}{\fs24 
. In addition to the affected characters, the DM can roll an item saving throw for the surrounding area; for example, if a stone room is furnished with tapestries and wooden benches, the DM can use the row for cloth and thin wood.
\par \tab If an item (or a victim\rquote s clothing, for example!) fails its saving throw, it may be }{\i\fs24 on fire}{\fs24 . People who catch fire suffer 1d4 points of damage in the following round and an additional die of damage in each subsequent round
\emdash for example 2d4 in the second round, 3d4 in the third round, and so on\emdash until they extinguish the flames. The victim must pass a saving throw vs. death magic in order to extinguish the flames and may gain a +2 to +8 bonus (DM\rquote 
s discretion) if he uses a sensible method for doing so.
\par \tab Large fires in confined areas (for example, dungeon rooms) can be especially dangerous since they deplete the oxygen in the air, making it unfit for breathing. If a fire fills more than 50% of an enclosed room or space, it fouls the air. Air\_
breathing creatures inside the room are affected as if the entire chamber had been targeted with a }{\i\fs24 stinking cloud }{\fs24 spell. After 1d6 rounds of ventilation (or 1d6 hours in an unventilated area), the atmosphere returns to normal.
\par \tab One more thing about big fires: they make things hot. Imagine a character\rquote s surprise when he goes to open the castle gates just after the wizard\rquote s }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 
goes off! In fact, the DM may rule that metal and stone objects retain enough heat to be affected as if they were struck with a }{\i\fs24 heat metal }{\fs24 
spell, although under most circumstances they will only be heated to the minimal damage level of the spell.
\par }{\fs28\cf6 
\par Cold}{\fs24 
\par \tab Cold\_based attacks are rarer than fire\_based attacks but are still fairly common among wizard spells. The most obvious examples are }{\i\fs24 ice storm, cone of cold, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 Otiluke\rquote s freezing sphere}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Visibility: }{\fs24 The supernatural temperatures of a cold\_based spell can cause some surprisingly thick fog by condensing water vapor in the air. The more humid the setting, the more likely it is for cold\_
based attacks to leave a residue of fog. Generally, cold spells create a cloud of fog equal in size to the spell\rquote s area of effect. The fog persists for 1d6 rounds, and any attacks in or through the mist suffer a \endash 2 attack penalty.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Environmental Effects: }{\fs24 Cold spells don\rquote t start fires, but they can leave a dangerous coating of ice on the ground; in fact, the }{\i\fs24 ice storm }{\fs24 
spell specializes in this effect. Creatures moving in the area of effect have a 25% chance of risking a slip or fall, which a saving throw vs. death magic (or a Dexterity check, DM\rquote 
s choice) can avert. Note that this is not as dangerous as the sleet application of }{\i\fs24 ice storm }{\fs24 since that version of the spell is designed to coat the ground with slippery ice.
\par \tab Objects subjected to intense cold may be weakened or become unusually brittle for a short time afterwards. Even if an item passes the saving throw, for the next 1d6 rounds it is especially vulnerable to normal blows or crushing blows, suffering a 
\endash 4 penalty on any further item saving throws.
\par \tab As noted under fires, metal or stone objects subjected to supernaturally cold temperatures may be affected as if struck by a }{\i\fs24 chill metal }{\fs24 spell.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Electricity and Lightning}{\fs24 
\par \tab The distinction between lightning and electricity is principally one of hitting power. A lightning bolt\rquote s immense voltage allows it to overcome the resistance of many things that normally don\rquote 
t conduct electricity at all. In addition, the power of a lightning bolt is so great that the target can blow itself to pieces as its fluids or water 
content is flashed to steam and then expands. This is why trees hit by lightning bolts split open; the water in the wood bursts the tree from inside as it turns to steam.
\par \tab In the AD&D magic system, }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt, call lightning, chain lightning }{\fs24 and blue dragon breath are lightning attacks. }{\i\fs24 Shocking grasp, glyphs of warding}{\fs24 
, and the touch of a volt, shocker, or electric eel are electricity attacks.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Visibility: }{\fs24 The bright arc of electricity or the flash of lightning as it strikes can temporarily dazzle people looking right at the bolt or spark, especially when the viewers\rquote 
 eyes are adjusted to darkness or dim light. In this case, the DM may require onlookers to make a saving throw vs. death magic or suffer a \endash 1 attack penalty for 1d3 rounds as their vision clears.
\par \tab In addition, lightning and electricity may start fires if applied to flammable targets (wood or trees usually, but not people). The smoke from a lightning\_struck fire can be just as disorienting as the smoke from a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Environmental Effects: }{\fs24 
Lightning and electricity leave few lingering effects behind; a stink of ozone (ionized air) may persist for 1d6 rounds, possibly confusing creatures that rely on smell to detect their prey, and objects may be }{\i\fs24 magnetized }{\fs24 
by their exposure to powerful electrical currents. This is not fantasy magnetism capable of picking up an armored warrior and hurling him through the air; this is low\_level magnetism capable of skewing compass needles and lodestones.
\par \tab Flammable items that fail their saving throw vs. lightning or electricity must make a second saving throw vs. normal fire or begin to burn. This may in turn cause smoke and other such effects.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Lightning bolts }{\fs24 cast underwater electrocute everything within a 20\_foot radius of the bolt\rquote s origin. Since the wizard
 can start the bolt up to 40 feet away from his position, he would be wise to do so when using this spell in an aquatic setting. If a caster out of the water throws a }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt }{\fs24 
into the water, the bolt has its normal effect on any creature in its path until it strikes the surface of the water; at that point, it expands in a 20\_foot\_radius globe in the water, and goes no further.
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 
\par Acid}{\fs24 
\par \tab Another unusual attack form is acid. Very few spells make use of acid as a weapon, although a fair number of monsters have acid\_based attacks, including the ankheg, the black dragon, and some slimes, puddings, and oozes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Visibility: }{\fs24 Acid at work tends to give off noxious fumes, usually in an area about 5 to 15 feet in diameter; the more acid, the larger the area affected. The fumes may obscure missile and melee attacks at the DM\rquote 
s option, creating a \endash 1 penalty for any creature trying to attack through the smoke and haze.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Environmental Effects: }{\fs24 Acid fumes are unpleasant to be around and may cause severe irritation of a victim\rquote 
s eyes, nose, and throat. If the DM desires, creatures within the cloud of fumes must roll saving throws vs. death magic or suffer a \endash 2 penalty to attack rolls and Armor Class as long as they remain nearby. This means that a character struck by }{
\i\fs24 Melf\rquote s acid arrow }{\fs24 may suffer combat penalties as well as damage from the spell.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Wind}{\fs24 
\par \tab Spells or effects that generate powerful gusts of wind can create a number of collateral effects. Enchantments such as }{\i\fs24 dust devil, gust of wind, wind wall, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 control wind }{\fs24 ar
e the most common in this group, and monsters such as djinni or aerial servants can create whirlwinds.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Visibility: }{\fs24 While a blast of wind doesn\rquote t necessarily obscure visibility, a gust on a very dusty or sandy surface is a different story altogether. On a dry, gritty surface, a wind\_
based spell or attack will create a rolling dust cloud that creates a \endash 2 penalty for people trying to fight or fire missiles into or through the area of effect.
\par \tab A blast of wind can also make it very difficult to hear anything above the roaring of the air and may carry a character\rquote s scent downwind and away from a creature that relies on smell or hearing to locate its enemies.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Environmental Effects: }{\fs24 Strong winds can create all kinds of havoc in a dungeon by extinguishing torches 
and candles and plunging the party into darkness. Above the ground, it can carry the sparks from a campfire farther than normal or blow over a lamp, possibly starting an unexpected fire.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Other Collateral Effects
\par }{\fs24 \tab Magic is astonishing and almost always has effects or uses that are not immediately apparent. Feel free to improvise when a player in your campaign tries something that seems especially clever\emdash 
or stupid. What happens when a purple worm is }{\i\fs24 polymorphed }{\fs24 into a puppy dog just after swallowing a still\_living character? Or how do the various rays of the }{\i\fs24 prismatic spray }{\fs24 
affect the area around them if they miss their target? What kind of wreckage does an}{\i\fs24  acid storm }{\fs24 spell leave behind when cast in a forest clearing?
 Be creative and take your best guess; the AD&D game is made of exceptions and special cases.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Critical Strikes  }{\fs24 Sometimes, a spell or a special attack strikes with such devastating effect that even the toughest opponent can be crippled or killed by a single blow. Critical strikes reflect this capability; even a lowly }{
\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 might strike the cyclops in his eye, while a }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt }{\fs24 that strikes a warrior dead\_on can kill him in an instant.
\par \tab Critical strikes add flavor and pseudo\_realism to the AD&
D game by increasing the likelihood that the use of a spell will decide a fight one way or the other. An AD&D combat can be reduced to a simple process of attrition, but the use of magic should create the potential for a battle\_
ending injury. Even if the evil giant has 90 hit points, and the wizard\rquote s }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 won\rquote 
t do more than 42 points of damage at its very best, a critical strike may allow that spell to severely wound or incapacitate the giant. After all, it\rquote s not very heroic to throw six volleys of }{\i\fs24 magic missiles }{\fs24 
into the same evil high priest; it denigrates the value of magic.
\par \tab Critical strikes can also represent special attack forms common among monsters. Creatures that spit acid, breathe fire, or exhale poison gas are just as likely to kill a hero as 
a creature that rends him limb from limb with fang and claw. A hell hound may only inflict 4 to 7 points of damage with its fiery breath, but that doesn\rquote t mean that a 60\_hit point fighter should not be threatened by the monster\rquote 
s attack! Critical strikes help to keep characters honest since }{\i\fs24 any }{\fs24 attack could result in severe injury or death.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 When Do Critical Strikes Occur?}{\fs24 
\par \tab The critical strike system presented here is meant to be an expansion of the critical hit system from }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics}{\fs24 . However, some of the mechanics that work for attack rolls and Armor Class can\rquote 
t be applied directly to saving throws. For a spell or an attack to have the potential for a critical strike, it must do physical damage. While it\rquote s easy to imagine a character who is double\_}{\i\fs24 charmed }{\fs24 or double\_}{\i\fs24 held}{
\fs24 , it\rquote s somewhat hard to extend the effects of such spells past the obvious results. After all, by failing the saving throw, the character already suffered whatever he was going to suffer for being subjected to the spell.
\par \tab Critical strikes occur when the target rolls a natural 3 or less on his saving throw }{\i\fs24 and}{\fs24 
 misses his saving throw by a margin of 5 points or more. If the spell in question does not allow a saving throw, the target still checks for a critical strike by ro
lling a saving throw versus the attack; this special saving throw has no other purpose than determining whether or not the character suffers a critical strike.
\par \tab High\_level casters or monsters are more likely to cause critical strikes, as shown on Table 45: Critical Strike Chance by Caster Level.
\par \tab Note that spells written on scrolls are assumed to be scribed at the 6th level of ability or one level higher than the minimum level required to cast them, whichever is higher; for example, a 5th\_level wizard spell requires a 9th\_
level caster, so it is scribed at the 10th level of ability, but a 1st\_level spell (requiring only a 1st\_level caster) is written at the 6th level of ability.
\par \tab Monsters with special attacks that may entail critical strikes use the table abo
ve, substituting their Hit Dice for caster level. As one might suspect, ancient red dragons of the 20 Hit Dice or higher variety can inflict devastating criticals with their fiery breath!
\par }{\i\fs24 
\par \tab Beshira the Sorceress, a 4th\_level mage, hurls a }{\fs24 magic missile }{\i\fs24 at an unfortunate orog. Normally, a }{\fs24 magic missile }{\i\fs24 
allows no saving throw, but the orog will still roll a d20 for a saving throw vs. spell just to see if he fails critically or not. The orog is a 3 Hit Die monster, which means that his saving throw vs. spell is a 16. The orog rolls an 8, which can\rquote 
t be a critical strike, since it\rquote s not a natural 3 or less\emdash  even though he missed his save by 8 points!
\par \tab A few levels later, Beshira encounters an angry cloud giant and decides that }{\fs24 lightning bolt }{\i\fs24 is the best way to d
eal with him. The cloud giant is a 16 Hit Die monster and only needs a 7 to make its saving throw. However, the giant rolls a 4, missing its save! Since Beshira is now an 8th\_level sorceress, this natural 4 may be a critical strike\emdash 
except the giant still didn\rquote t miss by 5 points. (He would have had to roll a 1 or 2 to miss his saving throw by a margin of 5 points.)
\par \tab Later in the same fight, Beshira exhausts her spells and hauls out a }{\fs24 wand of fire }{\i\fs24 to use against the giant. Against rods, staves, and wands, the giant\rquote 
s saving throw is a 6, so he will only suffer a critical strike on a roll of 1.
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Critical Strikes the Easy Way}{\fs24 
\par \tab If the math of the preceding system seems to be a little too much for your campaign, try this method: the attacker rolls 1d20 when he thr
ows the spell or uses his special ability. If the die comes up an 18 or higher, the victim suffers a critical strike if he fails his saving throw. If the spell or effect doesn\rquote 
t allow a saving throw, the victim may still attempt a saving throw vs. death magic to avoid the critical strike itself, although he still suffers the effects of the spell even if he avoids the critical hit. This system doesn\rquote 
t account for the skill or power of the spellcaster or monster, but it may be a little faster than figuring out how much someone missed their save by.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Types of Critical Strikes
\par }{\fs24 \tab Obviously, the exact nature of the injuries a character suffers from a critical strike will vary with the form of energy or magic that hits him. Different spells or effects can burn, char
, dissolve, electrocute, freeze, impale, maul, scorch, or wound the victim in different ways. Therefore, there are ten different critical strike charts, each representing a different attack form.
\par \tab Readers familiar with the critical hit system from }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 
may observe that the target type (humanoid, monster, or animal) is not a consideration in the critical strike charts. Instead, only one body form or target type is included for each attack. Optional hit location tables for monster
s and animals are provided in case you wish to incorporate them in your campaign, but you can use the critical strike tables for any kind of opponent as they are written.
\par \tab The critical strike tables include acid, cold, constriction, crushing, electricity, fire, impact, slashing, vibration, and wounding.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Acid }{\fs24 attacks include }{\i\fs24 Melf\rquote s acid arrow, acid storm}{\fs24 , the breath of a black dragon, the secretions of a black pudding, or the effects of holy water on certain undead.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Cold }{\fs24 attacks include }{\i\fs24 chill touch, cone of cold, Otiluke\rquote s freezing sphere}{\fs24 , white dragon or silver dragon breath, and the deathly touch of a lich.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Constriction }{\fs24 attacks are the forte of giant snakes, monsters with tentacles, and spells such as }{\i\fs24 Evard\rquote s black tentacles }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 Bigby\rquote s crushing hand}{\fs24 
. Anything that surrounds and squeezes the victim could fall into this category.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Crushing }{\fs24 attacks are mammoth assaults that can smash a person like a bug. }{\i\fs24 Turn pebble to boulder, Bigby\rquote s clenched fist}{\fs24 , or the blow from a }{\i\fs24 ring of the ram }{\fs24 are 
crushing attacks. Crushing can also represent the effects of a dangerous fall or the force of an explosion that hurls a character through the air. If the character\rquote s whole body is affected by a physical force, it\rquote s a crushing injury.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Electricity }{\fs24 includes various forms of magical }{\i\fs24 lightning, shocking grasp}{\fs24 , and the breath of a behir or blue or bronze dragon. There is no distinction between electricity and lightning for critical strikes.

\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Fire }{\fs24 is fairly obvious, but note that explosions (a gas spore explosion or the retributive strike of a broken staff) are more accurately described by crushing injuries.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Impact }{\fs24 injuries result from concentrated applications of force or small projectiles. }{\i\fs24 Magic missile, spiritual hammer, }{\fs24 or bullets cause impact injuries. In so
me cases, impact injuries actually penetrate the victim\rquote s body. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Slashing }{\fs24 attacks include spells such as }{\i\fs24 ice knife, Mordenkainen\rquote s sword}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 blade barrier}{\fs24 . This category also includes the mauling action of some mechanical traps.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Vibration }{\fs24 attacks are rare, but dangerous, literally shaking the victim to pieces.  A few monsters have special vibration or sonic attacks.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wounding }{\fs24 attacks result from }{\i\fs24 cause light, serious }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 critical wounds}{\fs24 , and can also simulate withering, desiccation, or disease attacks. Wounds appear as gaping sores or destroyed tissue.

\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Strike Location
\par }{\fs24 \tab In the movies, the hero always manages to get shot in the shoulder, the leg, or just grazed in the side. Regrettably, adventurers aren\rquote t always that lucky, and even a fairly minor critical strike\emdash say, a }{\i\fs24 magic missile}{
\fs24 \emdash could be extremely dangerous if it strikes the character in the eye or the throat. The location of a critical strike is determined by rolling a d10 and comparing it to Table 46: Critical Strike Location.
\par \tab For your convenience, the hit locations are numbered on each subtable in the critical strike charts.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Called Shots: }{\fs24 If a spell or special attack uses a normal attack roll, the attacker may choose to use a called shot. A called shot forces the attacker to take a \endash 4 penalty on his 
attack roll, but if it hits and inflicts a critical strike, the strike automatically affects the location the attacker was aiming for. Spells that do not use attack rolls do not permit the caster to attempt a called shot.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Whole-Body Injuries: }{\fs24 Unlike most weapons, spells and special attacks create the possibility for whole\_body injuries which damage a creature in several places at the same time. This is a function of the spell\rquote 
s size or area of effect.
\par 
