\par }{\fs28\cf6 Severity
\par }{\fs24 \tab Naturally, critical strikes cover a great range of severity. There\rquote s a world of difference between a }{\i\fs24 burning hands }{\fs24 spell cast by a 2nd\_level wizard and the awesome blast unleashed by an ancient, fire\_
breathing dragon. If the whole\_body injury rules cover the size of the attack, severity measures its intensity.
\par \tab The best approximation for the severity of a critical strike
 is how much damage the attack can inflict on its target. Figure the maximum damage potential of the spell or effect, compare it to the maximum, unwounded hit point total of the victim, and refer to Table 48: Critical Severity.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Terthan, the warrior from the previous example, has a normal hit point maximum of 35 when unwounded. The }{\fs24 lightning bolt }{\i\fs24 was thrown by a 6th\_
level wizard with a potential of 6d6 points of damage (a maximum of 36 points). Since this is more than Terthan\rquote s hit points, without being twice Terthran\rquote s total, the severity roll will be 2d6.
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Refer to the appropriate critical strike chart and roll the severity dice indicated for the hit location. The result is the specific injury or effect suffered by the victim for the critical strike. Unlike the critical hits of the }{\i\fs24 Player
\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 book, critical strikes do }{\i\fs24 not }{\fs24 inflict doubled damage, and they do not allow the victim a special saving throw to avoid the specific effects. After all, the target already blew one saving throw,
 or he wouldn\rquote t be rolling on the critical strike table.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Lord Nonnach Redoff the Bold, a 20th\_level warrior, rides forth to do battle with an adult red dragon. The dragon naturally opens the fray with its best fiery blast. Nonnach must make a saving thro
w vs. breath weapon, needing only a 4 to succeed. Unfortunately, Nonnach rolls a 1 and misses. Since the dragon has 17 Hit Dice, its victims suffer critical strikes if they miss their save by at least 3 points and roll less than a 5\emdash 
which Nonnach just did.
\par \tab Chortling with glee, the DM rules that the dragon\rquote s breath is a huge attack, inflicting critical strikes in 1d4 locations; Nonnach rolls a 3, so he\rquote 
ll get three separate rolls on Table 46 and winds up with critical strikes to the abdomen, right leg, and left arm. Now, on to severity: the dragon\rquote 
s damage potential is 126 points, and Nonnach at his best only has 109 hit points, so each strike will be rolled with a severity of 2d6. In addition to the 79 points of damage Nonnach takes from the dragon\rquote s breath
, he suffers minor injuries to his arm and abdomen, but his leg is burned off at the knee! (And the DM could require him to make a saving throw vs. death magic to check for death from massive damage, as described in the }{\fs24 PHB}{\i\fs24 
.) All of a sudden, Nonnach finds himself wishing he were somewhere else entirely.
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Specific Injuries and Effects}{\fs24 
\par \tab Usually, a critical strike results in a specific injury or penalty for the victim. As noted above, the victim does not gain a saving throw to avoid the special effects of a critical strike since he\rquote 
s already failed one pretty badly. However, many creatures can ignore the effects of certain strikes just by their nature. Most undead monsters are immune to cold\_based attacks and therefore don\rquote t suffer any ill effects from cold cr
itical strikes. Constructs such as golems don\rquote t have a real physiology and can ignore the incapacitating effects of internal injuries. Injuries are divided into six degrees of severity: }{\i\fs24 grazed, struck, injured, broken, shattered }{\fs24 
and finally }{\i\fs24 severed}{\fs24 . 
\par \tab Woun
ds or injuries are always accompanied by some loss of hit points, and some critical strike results may increase the damage caused by the spell or effect. Many also inflict temporary attack or movement penalties that persist until the character receives me
dical attention of some kind. The actual damage, or loss of hit points, may be recovered normally, but the injury\rquote s effects linger until it is specifically addressed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab After a few months of recovering from his battle with the dragon, Nonnach is riddled by a high\_level wizard\rquote s }{\fs24 magic missile }{\i\fs24 spell and receives a critical strike. }{\fs24 Magic missiles }{\i\fs24 
strike on the impact chart; the location roll is a 10 and the severity roll comes up as a 6, so Nonnach catches a }{\fs24 missile }{\i\fs24 in the eye. The spell inflicts 16 points of damage, but more importantly, Nonnach is temporarily blinded. He
\rquote ll suffer a \endash 4 penalty to all attack rolls until his injury is treated in some way, although he can recover the loss of hit points through magical or normal healing.
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Grazed, Singed, Chilled, Bruised: }{\fs24 While the nature of the injury varies with the type of critical strike, all of these minor wounds fall under the category of \lquote grazes.\rquote  A graze is not very ser
ious, although it may cause minor bleeding. A }{\i\fs24 cure light wounds }{\fs24 
or any other healing magic capable of restoring 4 hit points will heal a graze. Grazes also heal naturally as if they were a loss of 1d6 hit points; if a graze is the equivalent of a 2\_hit poi
nt wound, a single day of rest will cure it and eliminate any penalties. If a character receives healing magic, the graze is healed }{\i\fs24 and }{\fs24 he gets to recover hit points from the same spell or effect.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Struck, Blistered: }{\fs24 Slightly more serious than a graze, a \lquote struck\rquote  result usually entails some kind of attack or movement penalties, such as a \endash 
2 penalty to attack rolls. Injuries of this type can be healed by a }{\i\fs24 cure light wounds }{\fs24 spell or other healing magic capable of restoring 5 lost hit points. Struck areas heal naturally as if they were a wound of 2d6 hit points.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Injured, Burned, Frostbitten: }{\fs24 Wounds of this level can trouble a character for many weeks; they heal naturally as if they were a loss of 10d6 hit points. A }{\i\fs24 cure serious wounds }{\fs24 spell, or any other heal
ing magic capable of restoring 10 hit points or more, can repair the injury.
\par \tab Injuries almost always entail significant combat penalties for the character and may temporarily reduce a character\rquote s maximum number of hit points. Since he\rquote s favoring a painful 
injury, he is more susceptible to attacks. Injured arms, legs, or tails reduce a character to 75% of his normal maximum hit points, while an injury to the torso, abdomen, or head reduces a character to 50% of his normal. (If the damage done by the spell o
r effect didn\rquote t bring the character down to his new maximum, he is reduced to the lower number immediately, but if the damage was severe enough to drop the character beneath his new maximum, he doesn\rquote t lose any additional hit points.)
\par \tab For example, let\rquote s ta
ke a look at Lord Nonnach again. As noted in the example, Nonnach normally has a maximum of 109 hit points. If he sustained an injury to his torso, he would temporarily be reduced to a maximum of 55 hit points. Even if the torso injury only did 10 or 15 p
oints of damage, Nonnach is still going to lose half his hit points due to the effects of his injury!
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Broken: }{\fs24 Most minor breaks are assumed to fall into the previous two categories; hairline fractures or chipped bones are painful, but generally minor, inju
ries. This category is reserved for severe fractures that render the limb in question useless. Broken bones can be mended by a }{\i\fs24 cure serious wounds }{\fs24 spell that is used only to knit the bone; unlike the }{\i\fs24 grazed, struck}{\fs24 , or 
}{\i\fs24 injured }{\fs24 categories, this healing spell restores no hit points to the character. Broken bones heal naturally as if they were a loss of 20d6 hit points, so a character with a badly broken leg won\rquote 
t be running sprints anytime in the next month or two.
\par \tab Broken arms re
duce a character to 75% of his normal hit points, and broken ribs or legs reduce a character to 50% of his normal hit point maximum. Any other broken bones reduce a character to 25% of his normal hit point maximum. Continuing to adventure with a fractured
 skull or a few broken vertebrae is never a good idea.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Crushed, Shattered, Frozen, or Destroyed: }{\fs24 A critical strike of this magnitude simply destroys the affected tissue or limb, rendering it forever useless. The victim of such a devastating injury }{\i\fs24 never }{\fs24 re
covers his full health, without magical aid. A limb damaged in this way is completely useless, while other areas so affected will leave the victim incapacitated. One to eight months of bed rest will be required before he regains even a semblance of mobili
ty.
\par \tab An injury of this kind can only be repaired by a }{\i\fs24 cure critical wounds }{\fs24 
spell or any other magic capable of restoring at least 20 hit points to the victim. In addition, any bones in the affected area are assumed to be broken and may require additional magic to knit.
\par \tab Destroyed shoulders, hips, or limbs reduce a character to 50% of his normal hit point maximum. Any other injuries of this severity reduce the character to 25% of his normal hit point maximum. At the DM\rquote s option, appropriate ability scores may
 be reduced by up to 50%; an acrobat with a ruined hip will never perform again.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Severed, Dissolved, or Incinerated: }{\fs24 Obviously, a creature that has a limb severed or completely removed from his body can no longer use that limb for movement or combat. A human with a severed leg can\rquote 
t walk or run and is reduced to crawling until he finds a crutch; a character with a severed shield\_arm can no longer employ his shield, and so on. The only way to undo damage of this scale is by use of the }{\i\fs24 regeneration }{\fs24 spell or 
similar effects, or a properly worded }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 .
\par \tab The shock of losing a limb will prevent a character from moving independently or attacking for 2d10 full weeks, although a character who \ldblquote only\rdblquote  loses a hand or a foot may be able to perform limited activities wi
thin 1d6 rounds of the injury, if he passes a system shock roll. Note that creatures with more than four limbs (especially insects or octopus\_
like creatures) are slightly more resistant to this sort of trauma and may be able to continue the fight after one or two rounds of being stunned.
\par \tab The loss of a limb will reduce a character\rquote s hit point maximum by 25% for a partial loss, or 50% for a more catastrophic injury. If the character is able to compensate with a prosthesis such as a wooden leg or a hook, the 
hit point loss may be reduced by one level.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Bleeding}{\fs24 
\par \tab A common effect of many critical strikes is bleeding. Bleeding falls into three categories: minor, major, and severe. In many cases, bleeding may prove more dangerous than the original injury.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Minor bleeding }{\fs24 
causes the victim to lose 1d2 hit points per full turn until the wound is magically healed or bound. There is a chance that minor bleeding will stop on its own; every time the character suffers damage from the bleeding, he may attempt a saving thro
w vs. death magic. If he succeeds, the bleeding stops.
\par \tab Anybody (including the injured character!) can stop minor bleeding by applying a bandage. This requires one round.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Major bleeding }{\fs24 is much more dangerous, resulting in a loss of 1d2 hit points per rou
nd (not turn) until the injury is healed or bound. In effect, the standard negative 10 rule represents major bleeding; the character loses 1 hit point per round when reduced to negative hit points.
\par \tab Major bleeding can be stopped by a }{\i\fs24 cure light wounds }{\fs24 spel
l or the healing of 5 points of damage by any other means. In addition, the successful use of the healing proficiency halts major bleeding. If the wound is bound by an untrained character, make an Intelligence check for the would\_
be medic; if he fails, he is unable to help, but if he succeeds, the bleeding is reduced to minor.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Severe bleeding }{\fs24 causes the victim to lose 10% to 60% (1d6 x 10%) of his unwounded hit point total every round. For example, a fighter with 43 hit points would lose 4 (10%) to 24 (60%) of his hit points every round. A }{\i\fs24 
cure light wounds }{\fs24 or 5 hit points of healing will reduce severe bleeding to major bleeding; a }{\i\fs24 cure serious wounds }{\fs24 or 10 hit points of healing reduces it to minor bleeding; and a }{\i\fs24 cure critical wounds }{\fs24 
or 20 hit points of healing stops it altogether.
\par \tab An untrained character has no chance to bind a torso, abdomen, or head wound with severe bleeding, but a trained healer can attempt a proficiency check with a \endash 4 penalty to reduce severe bleeding to major bleeding in these areas
. A trained healer can reduce severe bleeding from a limb to major bleeding with an unpenalized proficiency check, and an untrained character can do the same with a successful Intelligence check at a \endash 
4 penalty. Note that once severe bleeding has been reduced to major bleeding, the character may be able to then reduce the major bleeding to minor bleeding.
\par \tab Bleeding from several wounds is cumulative; a character with three cases of minor bleeding from three separate injuries suffers 3d2 damage every turn. Af
ter a character is reduced to 0 hit points by any level of bleeding, the bleeding stops, and he simply loses 1 hit point a round until he reaches \endash 10 and dies. 
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Combat Penalties
\par }{\fs24 \tab Many critical strikes hamper the victim\rquote s ability to fight or move by penalizing his attack rolls or reducing his movement rate. A character with reduced movement may not choose to charge, jog, run, sprint, or force\_
march in order to compensate. A character reduced to no movement may still drag himself along with an effective movement rating of 1 or ride a mount with difficulty.
\par \tab A few critical strikes may prevent the victim from attacking at all; if this is the case, the character may not engage in spellcasting or performing any other actions except for using magical items or
 possibly using psionic powers that affect only his own body. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Knockdowns: }{\fs24 Some critical strikes may inflict a knockdown on the victim. This is not an automatic result; the victim may still attempt a saving throw vs. death magic to remain on his feet.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Armor, Shield, and Equipment Damage
\par }{\fs24 \tab Acid, fire, lightning, and other lethal energies and substances can quickly destroy a character\rquote s armor. Unlike the critical hits introduced in }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics}{\fs24 
, every critical strike endangers the victim\rquote s armor and equipment since the character already failed a saving throw. However, there\rquote s no need to check everything the character may have on his person\emdash 
instead, only check items on or in the location struck. For example, if a character is struck in the face with a }{\i\fs24 burning hands }{\fs24 
spell, his magical boots are in no real danger of being destroyed, but his helm or his reading spectacles might be ruined.
\par \tab To check for item damage, refer to Table 29: Item Saving Throws in the }{\i\fs24 DMG}{\fs24 . Vibration or sonic attacks ca
n be treated as either a crushing blow or disintegration as the DM prefers, although if you decide to use the disintegration column, a +4 bonus is reasonable to reflect the less destructive nature of vibration attacks. Wounding critical strikes do not dam
age equipment.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Partially Destroyed Armor: }{\fs24 If a character loses his armor in one particular location, the most accurate way to reflect his increased vulnerability is to consult the partial armor rules described on pages 128, 146 and 147 of the 
}{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 book. Basically, the character\rquote s overall AC will be worsened by the value of the missing piece, and the character will also have an AC 10 location that can be exploited by a called shot.

\par \tab If you don\rquote t have the }{\i\fs24 Combat & Tactics }{\fs24 book or don\rquote t want to use partial armor rules, simply assign the character a 1\_
point AC penalty for each piece of missing armor. For example, if a character in chain mail (base AC 5) loses the armor protecting his torso and right arm, his armor is now treated as a base AC of 7. This isn\rquote 
t as realistic, but may be a little easier to use in play.
\par 
