\par \tab Naturally, this spell is an improvement of the 3rd\_level alteration spell }{\i\fs24 blink}{\fs24 , allowing the wizard to shift his body to any point within 15 feet of his current location. Unlike the lower\_level spell, }{\i\fs24 improved blink }{
\fs24 allows the wizard to choose the exact time of his jump, the exact destination, and the orientation or facing of his ch
oosing. For example, a wizard confronted by an enemy fighter could blink just before the fighter attacked, reappearing directly behind his foe for a back attack. If the wizard blinks away from an attack, his enemy automatically misses\emdash 
but creatures with multiple attacks may be able to reposition themselves for another swing if the wizard blinks to a location within reach.
\par \tab If the wizard intends to take any action such as attacking, casting a spell, or using a magical item, he must decide before the round be
gins if he will do so before or after he blinks. If he acts before he blinks, he may be endangered by an attack before he finishes; he can choose to proceed with his action, hoping that he won\rquote 
t be hit, or he can abort his action by taking his blink for the round. On the other hand, if the wizard begins his action after his blink, the initiative modifier of his attack or spell is added to the time of his blink to determine when he attacks.

\par }{\i\fs24 
\par \tab A }{\fs24 blinking}{\i\fs24  wizard dueling a fighter decides to blink first, when the fighter attacks and then }{\fs24 lightning bolt }{\i\fs24 the offensive fellow. The fighter rolls a modified 6 for initiative, so the wizard waits until 6, then }{
\fs24 blinks}{\i\fs24 , making the fighter miss. At that time, he starts his }{\fs24 lightning bolt}{\i\fs24 , which has a casting time of 3\emdash the spell will go off on 9.
\par \tab In the }{\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat & Tactics }{\i\fs24 
initiative system, the wizard must count 1, 2, or 3 phases from the time of his blink for fast, average, or slow actions. If the fighter above attacked in the average phase, the wizard would blink during the fighter\rquote s attack, and then his }{\fs24 
lightning bolt }{\i\fs24 (a fast spell) would go off 1 phase later, with any other slow actions.
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Because the wizard can pick the location he is blinking to, he may not choose to blink into a movable object in order to force it aside\emdash 
he must blink to an area clear of obstructions or obstacles. If he does attempt to blink into a movable object, he will find himself displaced to a random location (use the blink 1d8 rules in the }{\i\fs24 PHB }{\fs24 for determining where he 
\par ends up).
\par 
