\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab A more potent form of the lesser }{\i\fs24 sign of sealing}{\fs24 , this spell allows the caster to guard an item or portal and prevent all other creatures from opening or passing through the sealed item or surface. The }{\i\fs24 greater sign }{
\fs24 has several effects; first of all, it affects a doorway or item that opens (a chest, for instance) as a wizard lock spell. If placed in an open corridor or archway to prevent passage, the }{\i\fs24 greater sign }{\fs24 
creates a magical barrier that repels all who try to pass.
\par \tab Second, the }{\i\fs24 greater sign }{\fs24 greatly strengthens the physic
al structure of any door or item it is placed upon, granting a +6 bonus on any item saving throws and allowing the item or door to ignore 1 point of damage per caster level from any attack. For example, a }{\i\fs24 greater sign }{\fs24 cast by a 12th\_
level wizard would reduce the damage of any blow or spell by 12 points, so a fighter armed with a broad sword (maximum damage of 8 points) could never hack through a door protected by the sign.
\par \tab Finally, if the protected doorway or item is forced open or destroyed by any means, 
the sign itself is not only destroyed, but also releases a spell upon the offending creature. The spell held by the sign is cast into the ward when the greater sign is created, and any spell the caster has memorized may be used in this way, from a }{
\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 shocking grasp }{\fs24 to a very nasty }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 polymorph}{\fs24 . The range of the sign\rquote 
s retributive spell is 10 yards per caster level, so it is possible to destroy the warding from a safe distance.
\par \tab The sign is displayed in plain sight, and most wizards will recognize it for what it is. The caster cannot specify particular creatures or conditions for the sign\rquote 
s operation; it functions against any creature that attempts to pass it, although the wizard can freely pass through his own sign without activating it. The }{\i\fs24 greater sign }{\fs24 
can be removed by the caster, thus ending the spell, or it can be defeated by a }{\i\fs24 limited wish }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 spell cast by a wizard of equal or higher level than the original caster; it cannot be dispelled.
\par \tab The material component for a greater sign is a powdered diamond worth at least 1,000 gold pieces.
