\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Related to both the }{\i\fs24 contingency }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 spells, }{\i\fs24 persistence }{\fs24 allows a wizard to cast a spell of 6th level or lower and then hold it until it is needed. There are two general uses for }{
\i\fs24 persistence: }{\fs24 to use a personal spell effect as needed up to the maximum duration of the }{\i\fs24 persistence }{\fs24 itself, or to prepare an instantaneous spell and hold it ready until the caster wishes to use it.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 A. Personal effect. }{\fs24 Any spell that augments the wizard\rquote s natural abilities\emdash }{\i\fs24 detect magic, protection from evil, jump, infravision, fly, wraithform}{\fs24 , or other caster\_affecting spells\emdash can be made 
}{\i\fs24 persistent }{\fs24 by use of this spell. The wizard casts }{\i\fs24 persistence }{\fs24 and then immediately follows with the desired spell. Instead of taking effect immediately, the magic of the }{\i\fs24 persistence }{\fs24 
holds it ready for use by a simple act of will. The wizard can then \ldblquote turn on\rdblquote  or \ldblquote turn off\rdblquote  the girded spell as often as he likes over the course of the duration of the }{\i\fs24 persistence}{\fs24 
. The duration of the girded spell only runs while the spell is active, so a 15th\_level wizard who makes a }{\i\fs24 fly }{\fs24 spell }{\i\fs24 persistent }{\fs24 will be able to use 1d6+15 turns of flight (the normal duration of fly) over the nex
t 15 days (the duration of the }{\i\fs24 persistence}{\fs24 ) as he sees fit.
\par \tab This is especially useful because it allows the wizard to cast the girded spell and the }{\i\fs24 persistence}{\fs24  and have the girded spell\rquote s effects available while he then memorizes another spell in place of the spell made }{\i\fs24 
persistent}{\fs24 . It is also useful because the girded spell can be activated instantly by an act of will. The main difference between this spell and }{\i\fs24 contingency }{\fs24 lies in the fact that a }{\i\fs24 persistent }{\fs24 
spell may be invoked several times (up to the limit of its normal duration) while a }{\i\fs24 contingency }{\fs24 functions once only.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 B. Held spell. }{\fs24 Spells that have an instantaneous effect, such as most attack spells and some movement spells like }{\i\fs24 teleport }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 dimension door}{\fs24 , can be rendered }{\i\fs24 persistent }{\fs24 
as well. The held spell may be activated or discharged at any time during the duration of the }{\i\fs24 persistence}{\fs24 , but its magic is then exhausted as if it had been cast normally. This resembles the effect of a }{\i\fs24 contingency }{\fs24 
spell, but the effect has no predefined conditions and simply occurs when the caster wills it to.
\par \tab Damaging or offensive spells that have a duration (for example, }{\i\fs24 flaming sphere or wall of fire}{\fs24 ) cannot be rendered }{\i\fs24 persistent}{\fs24 . A wizard may have no more than one }{\i\fs24 persistence }{\fs24 
spell active at any given time; if he girds a new spell while an old one is still }{\i\fs24 persistent}{\fs24 
, the old spell is simply replaced by the new one. The material component is a crystal chalice of exquisite workmanship worth at least 2,000 gp. The material component of a held or girded spell is expended when the spell is made }{\i\fs24 persistent}{
\fs24 .
