\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 Range: 0\tab Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 year\tab Casting Time: 1 day
\par Area of Effect: The caster\tab Saving Throw: None
\par Subtlety: 8\tab Knockdown: None
\par Sensory: None\tab Critical: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 \tab This potent spell exchanges the necromancer\rquote 
s own living heart for a finely crafted heart of perfect, unblemished stone that alters the very nature of the wizard\rquote s body. As long as the }{\i\fs24 heart of stone }{\fs24 remains i
n effect, the caster need not fear attacks that pierce, slash, or cut him; he does not bleed and can ignore the most horrible injuries of this kind. The caster can also ignore most magical effects such as spells, magical devices, and innate spell abilitie
s of less than 8th level. 
\par \tab In game terms, the character suffers only 1 point of damage from any type S or type P attack, plus any magical adjustment for the weapon. For example, if struck by a }{\i\fs24 long sword +1}{\fs24 , he would suffer only 2 points of damage. Strengt
h and specialization bonuses are ignored. If the wizard is dismembered, he suffers no additional damage other than the inconvenience of having his limbs removed, and he can reattach a severed limb by holding it in place for one full turn. The }{\i\fs24 
heart of stone }{\fs24 is also partially effective against type B attacks since it prevents bruising, swelling, and crushed blood vessels. Against bludgeoning weapons, the wizard only suffers half the normal damage. }{\i\fs24 Cause wound spells }{\fs24 
always inflict minimum damage against a wizard protected by this spell. While the caster may not feel a sword in his rib cage, any damage sustained interrupts spellcasting.
\par \tab In addition to resisting injury, the }{\i\fs24 heart of stone }{\fs24 also renders the character immune to fatigue and exhaustion, whether normal or magical. He also gains a +4 bonus to saving throws vs. petrification attacks.
\par \tab While the }{\i\fs24 heart of stone }{\fs24 is quite powerful, it has limitations. First of all, determined physical attack can eventually destroy the wizard despite his unnatural resistance to injury\emdash 
a mob of angry peasants with hatchets and spears can finish him off 1 point at a time if that\rquote s what it takes. Second, the }{\i\fs24 heart }{\fs24 
confers no protection against other attack forms, such as fire, electricity, cold, acid, and so on, although any bleeding caused by a burn is ignored. }{\i\fs24 Disintegration }{\fs24 
effects also affect the caster. Most importantly, the caster loses the ability to naturally recover from injury and no longer regains lost hit points with the passage of time. Healing spells, potions and items are reduced to their minimum effect, so a }{
\i\fs24 cure serious wounds }{\fs24 (2d8+1 hit points restored) would only return 3 hit points to a wizard protected by heart of stone. However, }{\i\fs24 limited wish }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 
can be used to restore 1 hit point per level of caster or all but 1d4 hit points, respectively.
\par \tab In addition to these disadvantages, }{\i\fs24 heart of stone }{\fs24 also renders the caster vulnerable in one other way: his own real heart can be destroyed, instantly slaying him. Naturally, the caster will want to take steps to hide 
and protect his true heart to prevent this from happening. The living heart continues to beat for the duration of the spell but requires no special receptacle or facilities to protect it\emdash the caster could leave it lying on the floor, if he wished.

\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 heart of stone }{\fs24 cannot be dispelled, although a more powerful negation magic such as }{\i\fs24 Mordenkainen\rquote s disjunction }{\fs24 can bring the spell to an end. }{\i\fs24 Stone to flesh }{\fs24 
also undoes the magic of the }{\i\fs24 heart of stone}{\fs24 . No matter how the spell is ended, the wizard\rquote s own living 
heart instantly returns to its proper place, and the stone heart appears wherever the living heart was kept. At this time, any injuries the wizard currently has are multiplied by 1d6 as the wounds begin to bleed again. For example, a necromancer who was i
njured for 6 points of damage instead suffers 6d6 when the spell ends.
\par \tab The material component for this spell is the stone heart itself. This must be a carved stone of quality (jade, obsidian, or gold\_veined marble would be appropriate) worth not less than 5,000 gold pieces. It must be prepared by use of the }{\i\fs24 
enchant an item }{\fs24 spell. The stone is not consumed at the spell\rquote s end and may be used again if it is undamaged.
