\par \tab The most humble of priestly spells is the orison, a brief prayer or invocation of a minor nature. Typically, priests learn a number of }{\i\fs24 orisons }{\fs24 
as acolytes or students in order to hone their spellcasting skills and emphasize concepts, ideals, or phrases of particular importance to the faith. Because an }{\i\fs24 orisons }{\fs24 is not even on par with other 1st\_
level magic, a priest memorizes a number of individual }{\i\fs24 orisons }{\fs24 equal to three  +1 per level (up to a maximum of nine) when he devotes a 1st\_level spell slot to }{\i\fs24 orison}{\fs24 . In other words, a 1st\_
level priest can memorize four }{\i\fs24 orisons }{\fs24 for one 1st\_level spell slot, a 2nd\_level priest can memorize five, and so on.
\par \tab Unlike }{\i\fs24 cantrip}{\fs24 , an }{\i\fs24 orison }{\fs24 must have a specific effect, although the priest need not decide which incantation he will use until he actually casts the spell. Regardless of the prayer chosen, the }{\i\fs24 orison
\rquote s }{\fs24 duration is never more than one round per level. Known }{\i\fs24 orisons }{\fs24 include the following:
\par 
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Alleviate: }{\fs24 A single creature suffering from nausea or pain is relieved of its discomfort. Magically induced nausea or pain is only alleviated if the victim passes a saving throw vs. spell with a \endash 2 penalty.
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Calm: }{\fs24 A single creature that has been startled or frightened is soothed. Victims suffering from magical fear may attempt a save vs. spell with a \endash 2 penalty to calm themselves.
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Clarity: }{\fs24 For the duration of the orison, the priest\rquote s speech is clear and free of impediment\emdash useful for readings from sacred texts and other such rites. Magical conditions such as }{\i\fs24 confuse languages }{\fs24 
cannot be overcome by this }{\i\fs24 orison}{\fs24 .
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Courage: }{\fs24 The priest gains a +1 bonus to his next attack roll, as long as the attack is made within the spell\rquote s duration.
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Guidance: }{\fs24 The priest gains a +1 bonus to a Wisdom or Intelligence check to determine the right course of action in a moral dilemma or puzzle.
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Healing: }{\fs24 By his touch, the priest may heal a creature of 1 point of damage.
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Magic sense: }{\fs24 If there is a persistent spell effect or magical item within 10 yards, the priest feels a recognizable tingle or sensation of some kind. He has no way to determine what item or spell may have caused the reaction.

\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Memory: }{\fs24 Any item the priest commits to memory during the spell duration is more completely and permanently learned; he gains a +2 bonus to any checks to recall the exact appearance, wording, or meaning of an item, text, or message.

\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Resistance to magic: }{\fs24 The caster gains a +1 bonus to his next saving throw against magic of any type, as long as it occurs during the }{\i\fs24 orison\rquote s }{\fs24 duration.
\par  \tab }{\b\fs24 Resistance to poison: }{\fs24 The priest gains a +1 bonus to his next saving throw vs. poison, as long as it occurs during the }{\i\fs24 orison\rquote s }{\fs24 duration.
\par \tab Other orisons of similar power or scope may be permitted by the DM. Generally, an }{\i\fs24 orison }{\fs24 should not affect more than one creature or die roll at a time, and an }{\i\fs24 orison }{\fs24 that can actually caus
e immediate harm to a creature should inflict no more than 1 or 2 points of damage. An offensive }{\i\fs24 orison }{\fs24 would be quite rare and most probably associated with an evil or chaotic priesthood.
\par 
