\par Sensory: Small audio\tab Critical: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Originally developed by the Harmonium faction of the Outer Planes, this useful spell has come into more widespread use in recent years. While the spell is available as a 2nd\_
level enchantment for members of the Harmonium, the general version is not quite as efficient and is considered a 3rd\_level spell.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 dictate }{\fs24 spell is an improved version of command, affecting up to 6 creatures in a 20\_
foot cube. The caster is not limited to a single word and can issue an order of no more than a dozen words in length. All the specified targets who fail their saving throws must attempt to obey the caster\rquote 
s instructions. For example, a priest could issue a dictate such as \ldblquote Stay here until I return,\rdblquote  \ldblquote Throw down your weapons,\rdblquote  or \ldblquote Seize that elf!\rdblquote 
 The subjects will continue to obey nonimmediate orders for up to one round per experience level of the caster.
\par \tab Subjects who cannot understand the caster are not affected, so characters who do not understand the caster\rquote s language are immune to this spell. In addition, the order must create an immediate and obvious course of action for the subject; a }{
\i\fs24 dictate }{\fs24 to \ldblquote Die!\rdblquote  or \ldblquote Feel sorry for him!\rdblquote 
 would simply cause the subject to stand still in confusion for one round. Poorly worded or confusing commands grant the subjects a +1 to +4 bonus on their saving throws at the DM\rquote 
s discretion. Similarly, if after the subject fails his saving throw he is given an obviously self\_destructive }{\i\fs24 dictate}{\fs24 , the subject simply loses his next round as he fights off the compulsion.
