\par \tab By anticipating possible futures and outcomes of the caster\rquote s actions, this spell provides the character with a temporary sixth sense or feel for danger. The spell operates on a subconscious level, and the cas
ter receives strong intuitive impulses when he contemplates courses of action that may bring immediate physical injury or harm to him. For example, if the priest was about to open a trapped chest, the }{\i\fs24 unfailing premonition }{\fs24 
would create a flash of insight or a gut feeling telling him that he shouldn\rquote t do so. Similarly, opening a door that leads into the lair of a ferocious troll may also trigger the spell\rquote 
s warning. Threatening a NPC who is likely to respond by drawing a weapon and attacking the PC would create a warning, but threatening a NPC who will get even with the priest in an hour or two will not trigger the }{\i\fs24 premonition.}{\fs24 
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 unfailing premonition }{\fs24 is also quite useful in combat, as long as the priest obeys his instincts and ducks, dodges, or withdraws 
when his subconscious tells him to. While the spell is in effect, the priest gains a +2 bonus to his Armor Class and saving throws, but in any given round there is a 25% chance that he will have to forego his intended action in order to obey the spell
\rquote s warning impulses.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 premonition }{\fs24 only works on actions undertaken by the priest himself. If his companion is about to pull a mysterious lever that will drop a 10\_ton block of stone on the priest, he receives no warning.
